EXAM_DATA.papers["2015-english-one"] = {"id":"2015-english-one","year":2015,"label":"2015 英语一","era":"modern","sourceUrl":"https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/kaoyan/paper/2015-english-one/","timeLimitMin":180,"scoreMode":"estimated","parts":[{"id":"section1","kind":"cloze","title":"Section I 完形填空","directions":"Directions: Read the following text. Choose the best word(s) for each numbered blank and mark A, B, C or D on the ANSWER SHEET . (10 points)","points":10,"passage":{"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section1-1","text":"Though not biologically related, friends are as \"related\" as fourth cousins, sharing about 1% of genes. That is   {{1}}   a study, published from the University of California and Yale University in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, has   {{2}}   ."},{"id":"p-section1-2","text":"The study is a genome-wide analysis conducted   {{3}}   1,932 unique subjects which   {{4}}   pairs of unrelated friends and unrelated strangers. The same people were used in both   {{5}}   ."},{"id":"p-section1-3","text":"While 1% may seem   {{6}}   , it is not so to a geneticist. As James Fowler, professor of medical genetics at UC San Diego, says, \"Most people do not even   {{7}}   their fourth cousins but somehow manage to select as friends the people who   {{8}}   our kin.\""},{"id":"p-section1-4","text":"The study   {{9}}   found that the genes for smell were something shared in friends but not genes for immunity. Why this similarity exists in smell genes is difficult to explain, for now.   {{10}}   , as the team suggests, it draws us to similar environments but there is more   {{11}}   it. There could be many mechanisms working together that   {{12}}   us in choosing genetically similar friends   {{13}}   \"functional kinship\" of being friends with   {{14}}   !"},{"id":"p-section1-5","text":"One of the remarkable findings of the study was that the similar genes seem to be evolving   {{15}}   than other genes. Studying this could help   {{16}}   why human evolution picked pace in the last 30,000 years, with social environment being a major   {{17}}   factor."},{"id":"p-section1-6","text":"The findings do not simply explain people's   {{18}}   to befriend those of similar   {{19}}   backgrounds, say the researchers. Though all the subjects were drawn from a population of European extraction, care was taken to   {{20}}   that all subjects, friends and strangers, were taken from the same population."}]},"questions":[{"n":1,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"when"},{"key":"B","text":"why"},{"key":"C","text":"how"},{"key":"D","text":"what"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"语法形态","stemZh":"1.","reason":"从句动词 concluded 缺宾语 That is __(1)__ a study … has __(2)__ 中，从句的主语是 a study、谓语是 has concluded（由第 2 题确定），concluded 在此为及物用法，需要一个宾语；而前句 That 指代 friends are as \"related\" as fourth cousins, sharing about 1% of genes 这一论断，正是研究“得出的结论”本身——从句缺的就是这个宾语。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"when 引导时间状语从句或表时间的名词性从句，但本句从句不缺成分中的时间状语，concluded 缺的是宾语而非时间，故排除。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"why 引导原因从句，从句内部需要完整；但本句 has concluded 缺的是结论本身，而不是“为何得出结论”，逻辑不合。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"how 引导方式从句，从句内部成分应完整；本句缺的是 concluded 的宾语（即结论内容），不是“如何得出”，方式义对不上。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"what 既能引导表语从句，又能在从句内部充当 concluded 的宾语，含义为“……所得出的结论”，正好回指前句 friends are as \"related\" as fourth cousins 这一研究结论，结构与语义双线对得上。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":2,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"defended"},{"key":"B","text":"concluded"},{"key":"C","text":"withdrawn"},{"key":"D","text":"advised"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"2.","reason":"上句提出研究的结论 Though not biologically related, friends are as \"related\" as fourth cousins, sharing about 1% of genes. That is __(1)__ a study … has __(2)__. 前句给出的就是一项研究“得出的结论”，所以本空动词应表“得出（结论）”。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"defended 意为“捍卫、辩护”，常用于受到攻击时的辩护；上文并未提及任何争论，纯属无中生有。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"concluded 意为“得出结论”，与前句“研究的结论”完全对位，是研究类文本最常见的搭配 a study has concluded that…，词义与语境双线契合。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"withdrawn 意为“撤回、撤销”，与文意正好相反——本研究是被刊出、被引述的，而不是被撤回。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"advised 意为“建议、劝告”，主语通常是人，study 本身并不“建议”，且全文并无建议性论述，逻辑错位。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":3,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"for"},{"key":"B","text":"with"},{"key":"C","text":"on"},{"key":"D","text":"by"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"3.","reason":"对 1932 名受试者展开的分析 The study is a genome-wide analysis conducted __(3)__ 1,932 unique subjects 表明这是一项“针对/基于 1,932 名独立受试者”的分析，研究英文里的固定搭配是 conduct an analysis/a study on sb。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"for 多表“为了……”，conduct an analysis for sb 一般指“为某人做”，但研究并非“为受试者做”，搭配与语义都不合。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"with 表伴随或工具，conduct an analysis with 强调“用……方法做”，后面常接工具或方法名词，不接受试者人群。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"on 与 analysis/study 搭配表“针对……展开”，conduct a study/analysis on subjects 是研究类文本的标准框架，对应 1,932 名受试者完全契合。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"by 引出施动者，conducted by 后面应是研究者；但本句 1,932 unique subjects 是受试者而非研究者，逻辑反了。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":4,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"compared"},{"key":"B","text":"sought"},{"key":"C","text":"separated"},{"key":"D","text":"connected"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"4.","reason":"朋友 vs 陌生人两组对照 a genome-wide analysis conducted __(3)__ 1,932 unique subjects which __(4)__ pairs of unrelated friends and unrelated strangers，研究把受试者分成“无血缘朋友对”与“无血缘陌生人对”两组——这是典型的对照实验结构，研究的目的就是把两组进行比较。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"compared 意为“比较”，与 pairs of unrelated friends and unrelated strangers 这一两组对照结构完美对位，体现研究设计的核心动作。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"sought 意为“寻找、追求”，本句受试者已经被选定（unique subjects），不再处于“寻找”阶段，逻辑链断裂。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"separated 意为“分开”，仅描述分组动作，没体现两组之间的对比关系，与实验“得出共享基因”的结论缺少桥梁。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"connected 意为“连接”，把朋友对和陌生人对“连接”起来在实验逻辑上不通——研究的恰恰是两组的差异，而不是把它们合在一起。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":5,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"tests"},{"key":"B","text":"objects"},{"key":"C","text":"samples"},{"key":"D","text":"examples"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"5.","reason":"受试者被用于两组 The same people were used in both __(5)__. 表“同一批人被用于两个 __ 里”，回指上文 pairs of unrelated friends and unrelated strangers 这两组受试者。研究学术语境中，“被分析的一组人”称为 sample（样本）。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"tests 意为“测试、考试”，但 The same people were used in both tests 隐含“做了两次测试”，本研究只是一次基因分析、并未做两次测试，对不上。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"objects 意为“物体、对象”，作“研究对象”用时英文一般作 subjects；objects 偏指物，不用于人，搭配错位。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"samples 意为“样本”，对应 pairs of unrelated friends and unrelated strangers 这两组样本群，是基因研究 / 统计学的标准说法，词义与术语都对得上。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"examples 意为“例子、范例”，常用于“举出例子”，不用于“研究样本”，缺少术语规范性。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":6,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"insignificant"},{"key":"B","text":"unexpected"},{"key":"C","text":"unreliable"},{"key":"D","text":"incredible"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"6.","reason":"but-句反转的对照 While 1% may seem __(6)__, it is not so to a geneticist. While …, it is not so 形成一组“普通人觉得 X、遗传学家不这么认为”的对照。普通人对 1% 的直觉是“少、不算什么”，遗传学家则觉得 1% 已不少——故 __(6)__ 应表“不重要 / 微不足道”。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"insignificant 意为“微不足道的、不重要的”，正是普通人对“仅 1%”的直觉，紧接 it is not so to a geneticist 正好形成“普通直觉 vs 专家观点”的反转，逻辑链最齐。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"unexpected 意为“出乎意料”，但后文遗传学家没有对“是否令人意外”做出反驳，反差句对不上，逻辑链断裂。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"unreliable 意为“不可靠”，对应的是数据可信度，而本句要描述的是“1% 给人的量级印象”，主体不对。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"incredible 意为“令人难以置信、惊人”，反倒像专家观点（1% 已经很多），与 while 引导的“普通人觉得”反向矛盾。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":7,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"visit"},{"key":"B","text":"miss"},{"key":"C","text":"seek"},{"key":"D","text":"know"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"7.","reason":"but 后对照朋友相似度 Most people do not even __(7)__ their fourth cousins but somehow manage to select as friends the people who __(8)__ our kin. but 前后形成对照：人们对四代表亲毫不“熟悉”，却选了与亲属相似的朋友。前半句强调“不知道”，与后半句“莫名地选了相似的人”形成反差。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"visit 意为“拜访”，是“认识之上”的更深一步，但本句要表达的是连最基本的“认识”都没有，层级用错。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"miss 意为“想念、错过”，“不想念远亲”虽然语法通，但与下文 select as friends the people who resemble our kin 的反差核心（“识 vs 不识”）对不上，色彩游离。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"seek 意为“寻找、追求”，与“普通人对远亲漠不关心”的语义不符——本句强调被动不识、而非主动寻找。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"know 意为“认识、了解”，do not even know their fourth cousins 表“连四代表亲都不认识”，与 but 后“却选了和亲属相似的朋友”形成最尖锐的“不知道 vs 莫名匹配”的反差，是英语口语 / 学术语境里描述这种“熟人 vs 陌生人”对照的标配。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":8,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"resemble"},{"key":"B","text":"influence"},{"key":"C","text":"favor"},{"key":"D","text":"surpass"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"8.","reason":"主旨：朋友像远亲 friends are as \"related\" as fourth cousins, sharing about 1% of genes 已点明全文主旨——朋友与远亲在基因上“相似”。本句 select as friends the people who __(8)__ our kin 是这一主旨的口语化表达，故 __(8)__ 应表“与……相似”。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"resemble 意为“与……相似”，直接对应主旨 friends are as \"related\" as fourth cousins，主旨复现最干脆。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"influence 意为“影响”，朋友“影响亲属”既偏题又与基因相似的主旨无关，方向跑偏。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"favor 意为“偏爱、有利于”，本句要表“相似”而非“偏爱”，语义错位。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"surpass 意为“超过、胜过”，朋友“超过我们亲属”与文意（朋友与亲属相似）完全相反。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":9,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"again"},{"key":"B","text":"also"},{"key":"C","text":"instead"},{"key":"D","text":"thus"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"9.","reason":"新增并列发现 a genome-wide analysis 与 1% may seem __(6)__, it is not so to a geneticist 已铺陈了第一项发现（朋友共享 1% 基因），The study __(9)__ found that the genes for smell were something shared in friends but not genes for immunity 又给出第二项发现（共享嗅觉基因、不共享免疫基因）——两项发现是并列追加关系，需要“也、还”这一类副词。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"again 表“又一次、再”，暗示“再做了一遍同样的事”，但本段是新的子发现，并非重复同一发现，逻辑色彩偏了。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"also 表“也、还”，恰好把“共享 1% 基因”和“共享嗅觉基因 / 不共享免疫基因”两项发现作为并列追加连接起来，是研究类文本最常用的并列推进副词。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"instead 表“反而、相反”，意味着否定前者改用后者；但本段并没有否定上文发现，反而是补充新发现，逻辑反向。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"thus 表“因此、所以”，但上文并未给出“嗅觉基因相似”的原因；两项发现之间是并列而非因果，关系错位。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":10,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Meanwhile"},{"key":"B","text":"Furthermore"},{"key":"C","text":"Likewise"},{"key":"D","text":"Perhaps"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"10.","reason":"as the team suggests 的猜测语气 Why this similarity exists in smell genes is difficult to explain, for now. __(10)__, as the team suggests, it draws us to similar environments but there is more __(11)__ it. 上句明确指出“为何嗅觉基因相似，目前难以解释”，紧接的 as the team suggests 表明这是研究团队的猜测，而非定论——因此 __(10)__ 应是“也许、可能”。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"Meanwhile 表“与此同时”，强调时间或事件并行；但本句是给出一种解释方向，不存在并行事件，逻辑错位。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"Furthermore 表“此外、而且”，用于在已有论据上追加新论据；但本句是对“难以解释”提出一种可能解释，不是追加新发现，方向不合。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"Likewise 表“同样地”，需要前后两件事类比同步；但前句是“难以解释”，本句是“也许出于环境相似”，不是类比，关系错位。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"Perhaps 表“或许、可能”，与 as the team suggests 共同体现“研究团队的猜测”基调，刚好回应上句 difficult to explain，逻辑链最干净。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":11,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"about"},{"key":"B","text":"to"},{"key":"C","text":"from"},{"key":"D","text":"like"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"11.","reason":"句型 there is more to it 的固定记忆 it draws us to similar environments but there is more __(11)__ it 是英语成语 there is more to it (than that) 的标准模板，意为“事情远不止这么简单 / 还有更深层的东西”。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"about 与 more about it 偏“更多关于它的内容”，搭配语义偏散漫，没有成语化的“事情不止于此”含义。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"to 构成 there is more to it 的固定句型，意为“事情不止于此、另有内情”，恰好为下文 There could be many mechanisms working together 提供铺垫。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"from 表“来自”，more from it 在英语里几乎不构成习语，搭配冷僻。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"like 表“像、似”,more like it 是另一个习语（“这才像话”），但意义跑偏，不能表达“事情不止于此”。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":12,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"drive"},{"key":"B","text":"observe"},{"key":"C","text":"confuse"},{"key":"D","text":"limit"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"12.","reason":"机制驱使我们择友 There could be many mechanisms working together that __(12)__ us in choosing genetically similar friends 这里 mechanisms 是抽象动因，承担“促使、驱动”我们做出择友行为的功能；典型搭配 mechanisms drive sb in doing sth。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"drive 意为“驱使、促使”，与 mechanisms 作主语、us 作宾语、in choosing 作伴随构成 drive sb in doing sth 标准框架，且与下文 \"functional kinship\" of being friends with __(14)__ 中“机制把我们引向相似朋友”的逻辑一致。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"observe 意为“观察”，主语应是有意识的观察者；mechanisms 作“观察者”主语错位，且与“促使我们择友”的句意不合。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"confuse 意为“使迷惑”，但本文整体是“机制把我们准确导向相似朋友”，并非“使我们迷惑”，色彩反向。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"limit 意为“限制”，与“机制促使我们做出某种选择”的积极推动语义相反，逻辑不通。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":13,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"according to"},{"key":"B","text":"rather than"},{"key":"C","text":"regardless of"},{"key":"D","text":"along with"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"13.","reason":"前后两端是两种不同择友动因 There could be many mechanisms working together that __(12)__ us in choosing genetically similar friends __(13)__ \"functional kinship\" of being friends with __(14)__ 空 13 把前后两端连起来：前端是 choosing genetically similar friends（选基因相似的朋友，基因层动因），后端是 functional kinship of being friends with（和能带来好处的人做朋友，功能性层动因）。作者在这里做对照——许多机制共同驱使（空 12 填 drive）我们选基因相似的朋友，而不是出于“功能性亲缘”。空处需一个表“是前者而非后者”的取舍连词。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"according to 表“根据”，常接出处或依据；但本句两端是两种动因的对比，并非依据某来源，关系错位。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"rather than 表“是 A 而不是 B”，正好对应作者“机制驱使我们因基因相似择友，而不是为了功能性亲缘的好处”这一对照取舍，是本文“机制层面 vs 功能层面”两种解释的核心切分线。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"regardless of 表“不顾、不管”，本句作者并没有“忽略功能性亲缘”，只是说基因相似才是主要动因，关系过强。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"along with 表“连同、和……一起”，但前后两个动因是对立解释而非并列共存，搭配错位。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":14,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"chances"},{"key":"B","text":"responses"},{"key":"C","text":"missions"},{"key":"D","text":"benefits"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"14.","reason":"功能性亲缘 = 朋友带来好处 \"functional kinship\" of being friends with __(14)__ 这里 functional kinship（功能性亲缘）指“朋友因互相能带来好处而被当作亲缘”，因此 __(14)__ 应表“好处、利益”，与英语口语 friends with benefits 暗合。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"chances 意为“机会”，但 functional kinship 强调“互利”而非“获得机会”，语义对不上。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"responses 意为“回应、反应”，与“功能性亲缘”完全无对应，无中生有。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"missions 意为“任务、使命”，friends with missions 既不构成英语习语，也与“互利”语义无关。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"benefits 意为“益处、好处”，friends with benefits 既是英语熟语，又精准回应 functional kinship 的“以利益为纽带”含义，是双线对位的最佳选项。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":15,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"later"},{"key":"B","text":"slower"},{"key":"C","text":"faster"},{"key":"D","text":"earlier"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"15.","reason":"下句 picked pace 暗示加快 Studying this could help __(16)__ why human evolution picked pace in the last 30,000 years 紧接着用 picked pace（加快步伐）来说明人类进化在过去 3 万年中加速；既然要解释“加速”，那么相似基因本身就应是“进化得更快”的那部分，空 __(15)__ 应表“更快”。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"later 表时间“更晚”，与下文 picked pace 描述的“加速”方向无关，关系不对。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"slower 表“更慢”，与下句“加快”完全相反，逻辑链断裂。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"faster 表“更快”，与下文 picked pace 的“加快进化步伐”对仗工整：相似基因进化得比其他基因快，因此能解释人类整体进化加速。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"earlier 表时间“更早”，描述“开始时间”而非“进化速度”，下文讨论的是节奏快慢而非起点早晚，方向错位。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":16,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"forecast"},{"key":"B","text":"remember"},{"key":"C","text":"understand"},{"key":"D","text":"express"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"16.","reason":"研究的作用是解释 Studying this could help __(16)__ why human evolution picked pace in the last 30,000 years，研究本身的功用就是帮助科学家“弄懂、理解”人类进化为何加速；这与全文研究类文本的标准语义对应。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"forecast 意为“预测”，对象通常是未来；但 picked pace in the last 30,000 years 是过去事件，无法被预测，时态指向错位。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"remember 意为“记得”，主语应是有记忆的人；研究“帮助记住原因”在英语中近乎不通，搭配不合。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"understand 意为“理解”，help us understand why … 是科研类文本最标准的句式，精准表达“研究帮助解开过去事件的原因”。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"express 意为“表达”，宾语是观点或情感；表达“原因”本身没有这种搭配，逻辑不通。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":17,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"unpredictable"},{"key":"B","text":"contributory"},{"key":"C","text":"controllable"},{"key":"D","text":"disruptive"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"17.","reason":"社会环境推动进化加速 Studying this could help __(16)__ why human evolution picked pace in the last 30,000 years, with social environment being a major __(17)__ factor. 整段在解释“为什么人类进化加速”，并把 social environment 列为主要原因——它是“推动进化加速”的贡献因素，因此 __(17)__ 应表“有贡献的、起作用的”。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"unpredictable 意为“无法预测的”，把社会环境说成“不可预测因素”会与作者“社会环境是主要推动因素”的清晰立场冲突，逻辑反向。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"contributory 意为“起促进作用的、起贡献的”，与 a major factor 同步强调社会环境对人类进化加速的推动作用，与作者立场契合。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"controllable 意为“可控的”，本段并未讨论社会环境是否可控，主题错位。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"disruptive 意为“破坏性的”，把社会环境说成对进化“破坏性”反而否定其推动作用，色彩反向。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":18,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"endeavor"},{"key":"B","text":"decision"},{"key":"C","text":"arrangement"},{"key":"D","text":"tendency"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"18.","reason":"群体性的择友模式 The findings do not simply explain people's __(18)__ to befriend those of similar __(19)__ backgrounds，这里描述的是“人们倾向于和背景相似的人做朋友”这一群体性、无意识的择友模式——它不是一次有意决策或安排，而是一种 tendency。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"endeavor 意为“努力、奋斗”，暗含“刻意付出”，但人们和相似背景的人做朋友是自然偏好而非刻意努力，色彩偏强。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"decision 意为“决定”，强调有意识的选择；本句强调的是潜意识的群体偏好，不是单一决定。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"arrangement 意为“安排”，强调外部设计或事先布置；交朋友是自发行为，搭配不合。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"tendency 意为“倾向、趋势”，恰好对应“人们倾向于和背景相似的人做朋友”这一普遍且无意识的群体偏好，且 tendency to do sth 搭配标准。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":19,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"political"},{"key":"B","text":"religious"},{"key":"C","text":"ethnic"},{"key":"D","text":"economic"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"19.","reason":"欧洲血统 = 族裔背景 all the subjects were drawn from a population of European extraction，extraction 意为“血统、出身”，directly 对应 ethnic（族裔的）；下句正是上句的同义复现，构成“族裔背景”暗号。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"political 意为“政治的”，与下句 European extraction（欧洲血统）没有同义关系，无中生有。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"religious 意为“宗教的”，与“欧洲血统”无关，且全文未谈宗教因素，关系断裂。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"ethnic 意为“种族的、族裔的”，与下句 European extraction 构成同义复现，是作者澄清“我们的发现不仅仅解释族裔相似择友”的精准切入点。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"economic 意为“经济的”，与下句 European extraction 的血统线索没有任何对应，关系错位。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":20,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"see"},{"key":"B","text":"show"},{"key":"C","text":"prove"},{"key":"D","text":"tell"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"20.","reason":"确保受试同源——研究规范的语义 care was taken to __(20)__ that all subjects, friends and strangers, were taken from the same population 表“采取措施以确保所有受试者来自同一群体”，是研究规范类表达。这里 __(20)__ 与 that 从句配合表“确保”——典型句型 see to it that / see that = 确保。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"see 与 that 从句构成 see (to it) that 这一固定搭配，意为“确保、注意确认”，与 care was taken to see that 的研究规范句型完美对位。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"show 意为“展示、证明给……看”，强调对外展示一项已经成立的事实，但本句要表达“确保事实成立”，方向不同。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"prove 意为“证明”，是事后逻辑推导；而 care was taken 强调的是事前采取措施，时间次序不对。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"tell 意为“告知、辨别”，与 care was taken to tell that 不构成英语习惯搭配，且语义偏离“确保”。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-1","kind":"reading","title":"Text 1","textNo":1,"directions":"Directions: Read the following four texts. Answer the questions after each text by choosing A, B, C or D. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET . (40 points)","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 1","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-1","text":"King Juan Carlos of Spain once insisted \"kings don't abdicate, they die in their sleep.\" But embarrassing scandals and the popularity of the republican left in the recent Euro-elections have forced him to eat his words and stand down. So, does the Spanish crisis suggest that monarchy is seeing its last days? Does that mean the writing is on the wall for all European royals, with their magnificent uniforms and majestic lifestyles?"},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-2","text":"The Spanish case provides arguments both for and against monarchy. When public opinion is particularly polarised, as it was following the end of the Franco regime, monarchs can rise above \"mere\" politics and \"embody\" a spirit of national unity."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-3","text":"It is this apparent transcendence of politics that explains monarchs' continuing popularity as heads of state. And so, the Middle East excepted, Europe is the most monarch-infested region in the world, with 10 kingdoms (not counting Vatican city and Andorra). But unlike their absolutist counterparts in the Gulf and Asia, most royal families have survived because they allow voters to avoid the difficult search for a non-controversial but respected public figure."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-4","text":"Even so, kings and queens undoubtedly have a downside. Symbolic of national unity as they claim to be, their very history – and sometimes the way they behave today – embodies outdated and indefensible privileges and inequalities. At a time when Thomas Piketty and other economists are warning of rising inequality and the increasing power of inherited wealth, it is bizarre that wealthy aristocratic families should still be the symbolic heart of modern democratic states."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-5","text":"The most successful monarchies strive to abandon or hide their old aristocratic ways. Princes and princesses have day-jobs and ride bicycles, not horses (or helicopters). Even so, these are wealthy families who party with the international 1%, and media intrusiveness makes it increasingly difficult to maintain the right image."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-6","text":"While Europe's monarchies will no doubt be smart enough to survive for some time to come, it is the British royals who have most to fear from the Spanish example."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-7","text":"It is only the Queen who has preserved the monarchy's reputation with her rather ordinary (if well-heeled) granny style. The danger will come with Charles, who has both an expensive taste of lifestyle and a pretty hierarchical view of the world. He has failed to understand that monarchies have largely survived because they provide a service–as non-controversial and non-political heads of state. Charles ought to know that as English history shows, it is kings, not republicans, who are the monarchy's worst enemies."}]},"questions":[{"n":21,"stem":"According to the first two paragraphs, King Juan Carl of Spain____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"eased his relationship with his rivals."},{"key":"B","text":"used to enjoy high public support."},{"key":"C","text":"was unpopular among European royals."},{"key":"D","text":"ended his reign in embarrassment."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据前两段,西班牙国王胡安·卡洛斯____","choicesZh":{"A":"缓和了他与对手的关系。","B":"曾经享有很高的公众支持。","C":"在欧洲王室中不受欢迎。","D":"在尴尬中结束了他的统治。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 According to the first two paragraphs 是典型的细节题问法，要求回到指定段落里抓事实。","parse":"题干 According to the first two paragraphs, King Juan Carl of Spain____ 是不完整陈述句，空白处由四个选项补完，主语是 King Juan Carl of Spain，谓语在选项里。结构平铺，无陷阱。","locateEn":"But embarrassing scandals and the popularity of the republican left in the recent Euro-elections have forced him to eat his words and stand down.","locateZh":"但近来欧洲议会选举中难堪的丑闻和共和派左翼的高人气，迫使他自食其言、宣告退位。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文从未提 Carlos 与对手关系'缓和'，只说他被对手（共和左翼）压力所迫退位，谈不上 eased his relationship with his rivals。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文说他被迫退位是因为反对派（republican left）人气高、自己陷丑闻，恰恰反衬其公众支持下滑；used to enjoy high public support 把作者批评的事实硬翻成正面叙述。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文谈 Carlos 在西班牙国内不得人心、被迫退位，从未把他与'其他欧洲王室'做受欢迎度对比，was unpopular among European royals 凭空造出王室同行的厌恶。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"D 项同时复现了'退位'和'难堪'两个要素，与预判完全吻合，而其他三项要么改变事实、要么无中生有。","pairs":[{"orig":"embarrassing scandals","opt":"in embarrassment"},{"orig":"stand down","opt":"ended his reign"}]}}}}},{"n":22,"stem":"Monarchs are kept as heads of state in Europe mostly____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"to give voters more public figures to look up to."},{"key":"B","text":"to achieve a balance between tradition and reality."},{"key":"C","text":"owing to their undoubted and respectable status."},{"key":"D","text":"due to their everlasting political embodiment."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"细节题","stemZh":"君主在欧洲被保留为国家元首主要是____","choicesZh":{"A":"为了给选民提供更多可以敬仰的公众人物。","B":"为了在传统与现实之间实现平衡。","C":"由于他们毋庸置疑且令人尊敬的地位。","D":"由于他们永恒的政治象征意义。"},"analysis":{"type":"Monarchs are kept as heads of state in Europe mostly____ 问的是客观原因，不带 infer/suggest，答案要在原文有直接出处。","parse":"Monarchs are kept as heads of state in Europe mostly____ 含被动语态 'are kept'，逻辑主语是隐含的'欧洲社会/选民'，副词 'mostly' 修饰整个原因，选项各以介词短语补出主要原因，结构清晰。","locateEn":"But unlike their absolutist counterparts in the Gulf and Asia, most royal families have survived because they allow voters to avoid the difficult search for a non-controversial but respected public figure.","locateZh":"但与海湾和亚洲的绝对君主不同，大多数王室之所以延续下来，是因为它们让选民免去了寻找一位无争议又受尊敬的公众人物的难题。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文说王室让选民 avoid the difficult search for a non-controversial but respected public figure——是'省去找别人'；to give voters more public figures to look up to 反过来说成'给选民提供更多可仰望的公众人物'，把'减少负担'偷换成'增加选项'。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文从未谈及王室在'传统与现实'之间求平衡，to achieve a balance between tradition and reality 是常识化的漂亮话，但无原文出处。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"C 项把原文'无争议+受尊敬'的两个属性合并为'undoubted and respectable'，并用 'owing to' 替换 'because'，预判要点全部落点。","pairs":[{"orig":"a non-controversial but respected public figure","opt":"their undoubted and respectable status"},{"orig":"most royal families have survived because","opt":"owing to"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文只说王室能 apparent transcendence of politics（看起来超越政治），强调的是'非政治化、无争议'；everlasting political embodiment（永恒的政治化身）反而把它写成强政治符号，且'everlasting'是无据极端化。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":23,"stem":"Which of the following is shown to be odd, according to Paragraph 4?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Aristocrats' excessive reliance on inherited wealth."},{"key":"B","text":"The role of the nobility in modern democracies."},{"key":"C","text":"The simple lifestyle of the aristocratic families."},{"key":"D","text":"The nobility's adherence to their privileges."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据第4段,下列哪一项被认为是奇怪的?","choicesZh":{"A":"贵族对继承财富的过度依赖。","B":"贵族在现代民主国家中的角色。","C":"贵族家庭简朴的生活方式。","D":"贵族对其特权的坚守。"},"analysis":{"type":"Which of the following is shown to be odd, according to Paragraph 4? 要求在指定段中找作者明确指出'odd'的对象，是细节定位题。","parse":"Which of the following is shown to be odd, according to Paragraph 4? 是被动语态的细节问句，被动主语 'which' 指四个选项，'is shown to be odd' 意为'被说成是奇怪的'，注意 'shown' 的施事是作者。","locateEn":"At a time when Thomas Piketty and other economists are warning of rising inequality and the increasing power of inherited wealth, it is bizarre that wealthy aristocratic families should still be the symbolic heart of modern democratic states.","locateZh":"在 Piketty 等经济学家正警告不平等加剧、世袭财富势力增强的当下，富有的贵族家庭居然仍是现代民主国家的象征核心，这件事很奇怪。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文里'依赖世袭财富'的主语是 inherited wealth 的力量在整个社会增强（Piketty 的警告），而非贵族在'过度依赖继承财富'。Aristocrats' excessive reliance on inherited wealth 把社会现象硬安到贵族头上，且加了 'excessive' 字眼，原文无此评价。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"B 项把 wealthy aristocratic families 上位为 nobility、把 symbolic heart 名词化为 role，原句的主谓两个要素全部落点。","pairs":[{"orig":"wealthy aristocratic families","opt":"The nobility"},{"orig":"the symbolic heart of modern democratic states","opt":"role ... in modern democracies"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文紧接着的 P5 才提到一些王室努力 strive to abandon or hide their old aristocratic ways，且这是讲'王室'而非泛指贵族；P4 谈贵族家庭，与'simple lifestyle'完全相反。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文确实在 P4 提到 outdated and indefensible privileges and inequalities，但作者用 bizarre 评价的是'贵族仍是民主国家象征核心'这件事，不是'贵族死抱特权'本身；D 把作者真正觉得奇怪的对象偏挪了。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":24,"stem":"The British royals \"have most to fear\" because Charles____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"fails to adapt himself to his future role."},{"key":"B","text":"fails to change his lifestyle as advised."},{"key":"C","text":"takes republicans as his potential allies."},{"key":"D","text":"takes a tough line on political issues."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"细节题","stemZh":"英国王室\"最需要担心\"是因为查尔斯____","choicesZh":{"A":"未能使自己适应未来的角色。","B":"未能按建议改变他的生活方式。","C":"将共和派视为他的潜在盟友。","D":"在政治问题上采取强硬立场。"},"analysis":{"type":"The British royals \"have most to fear\" because Charles____ 的 because 已经指明要在原文找因果链中'果'对应的'因'，是细节题。","parse":"The British royals \"have most to fear\" because Charles____ 是 because 引导原因状语从句的不完整句，主句已给'英国王室最该担心'，从句的谓语在选项里。注意 'have most to fear' 是 'have most (something) to fear' 的省略，意为'最有可担心的'。","locateEn":"He has failed to understand that monarchies have largely survived because they provide a service–as non-controversial and non-political heads of state.","locateZh":"他没能明白：君主制之所以基本上能存续，是因为它们提供了一项服务——担任无争议、非政治化的国家元首。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"A 项同时点中 Charles'认知缺失（fails to adapt = failed to understand 的结果）'和'未来角色（future role = non-political head of state）'两个要素，与预判一致。","pairs":[{"orig":"He has failed to understand that monarchies have largely survived because they provide a service","opt":"fails to adapt himself to his future role"},{"orig":"as non-controversial and non-political heads of state","opt":"his future role"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文从未提到任何人'建议'Charles 改变生活方式，fails to change his lifestyle as advised 凭空多出'as advised'这一情节。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文结尾 it is kings, not republicans, who are the monarchy's worst enemies 把共和派和国王对立，国王自己才是君主制的最大威胁；takes republicans as his potential allies 反把共和派写成 Charles 的潜在盟友，与原文极性相反。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文只说 Charles 有 a pretty hierarchical view of the world，谈的是世界观'等级化'，并未提他对具体政治议题持强硬立场；takes a tough line on political issues 凭空给他造'政治议题上的强硬路线'。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":25,"stem":"Which of the following is the best title of the text?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Carlos, Glory and Disgrace Combined"},{"key":"B","text":"Charles, Anxious to Succeed to the Throne"},{"key":"C","text":"Carlos, a Lesson for All European Monarchs"},{"key":"D","text":"Charles, Slow to React to the Coming Threats"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"主旨题","stemZh":"下列哪一项是本文的最佳标题?","choicesZh":{"A":"卡洛斯,荣耀与耻辱并存","B":"查尔斯,急于继承王位","C":"卡洛斯,所有欧洲君主的一课","D":"查尔斯,对即将到来的威胁反应迟缓"},"analysis":{"type":"Which of the following is the best title of the text? 是标题题，属主旨题特殊路径，要走第五步'串线—预判—比对'流程。","parse":"Which of the following is the best title of the text? 是标准疑问句，主语 'which'，'best title of the text' 即'本文最佳标题'，结构无陷阱。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"Carlos, Glory and Disgrace Combined 只覆盖 P1—P2 中 Carlos 个人'荣辱并存'的细节，没有触及全文 P3—P7 的'欧洲整体训诫'主线，标题主体局限到了人物本身。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文从未说 Charles Anxious to Succeed to the Throne（急于继位），只批他奢侈、等级观念重、没明白王室的服务本质；'anxious to succeed' 是凭空臆造的心理。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"C 项的'Carlos'对接 P1—P2 与 P6 的核心人物，'a Lesson for All European Monarchs'对接 P3—P5 的全欧视角与 P7 末句的训诫意味，主体与立场双双覆盖。","pairs":[{"orig":"it is the British royals who have most to fear from the Spanish example","opt":"a Lesson for All European Monarchs"},{"orig":"King Juan Carlos of Spain","opt":"Carlos"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"Charles, Slow to React to the Coming Threats 只覆盖 P6—P7 中 Charles 的局部内容，且'Slow to React'（反应迟钝）并不准确——原文说他是 has failed to understand（根本没理解），更不是'反应慢'；同时标题完全丢失 P1—P5 的内容。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-2","kind":"reading","title":"Text 2","textNo":2,"directions":"","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 2","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-1","text":"Just how much does the Constitution protect your digital data? The Supreme Court will now consider whether police can search the contents of a mobile phone without a warrant if the phone is on or around a person during an arrest."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-2","text":"California has asked the justices to refrain from a sweeping ruling, particularly one that upsets the old assumption that authorities may search through the possessions of suspects at the time of their arrest. It is hard, the state argues, for judges to assess the implications of new and rapidly changing technologies."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-3","text":"The court would be recklessly modest if it followed California's advice. Enough of the implications are discernable, even obvious, so that the justices can and should provide updated guidelines to police, lawyers and defendants."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-4","text":"They should start by discarding California's lame argument that exploring the contents of a smartphone – a vast storehouse of digital information – is similar to, say, going through a suspect's purse. The court has ruled that police don't violate the Fourth Amendment when they go through the wallet or pocketbook of an arrestee without a warrant. But exploring one's smartphone is more like entering his or her home. A smartphone may contain an arrestee's reading history, financial history, medical history and comprehensive records of recent correspondence. The development of \"cloud computing\", meanwhile, has made that exploration so much the easier."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-5","text":"Americans should take steps to protect their digital privacy. But keeping sensitive information on these devices is increasingly a requirement of normal life. Citizens still have a right to expect private documents to remain private and protected by the Constitution's prohibition on unreasonable searches."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-6","text":"As so often is the case, stating that principle doesn't ease the challenge of line-drawing. In many cases, it would not be overly burdensome for authorities to obtain a warrant to search through phone contents. They could still invalidate Fourth Amendment protections when facing severe, urgent circumstances, and they could take reasonable measures to ensure that phone data are not erased or altered while waiting for a warrant. The court, though, may want to allow room for police to cite situations where they are entitled to more freedom."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-7","text":"But the justices should not swallow California's argument whole. New, disruptive technology sometimes demands novel applications of the Constitution's protections. Orin Kerr, a law professor, compares the explosion and accessibility of digital information in the 21st century with the establishment of automobile use as a virtual necessity of life in the 20th: The justices had to specify novel rules for the new personal domain of the passenger car then; they must sort out how the Fourth Amendment applies to digital information now."}]},"questions":[{"n":26,"stem":"The Supreme Court will work out whether, during an arrest, it is legitimate to____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"prevent suspects from deleting their phone contents."},{"key":"B","text":"search for suspects' mobile phones without a warrant."},{"key":"C","text":"check suspects' phone contents without being authorized."},{"key":"D","text":"prohibit suspects from using their mobile phones."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"细节题","stemZh":"最高法院将裁定在逮捕过程中是否有正当理由","choicesZh":{"A":"阻止嫌疑人删除其手机内容。","B":"在没有搜查令的情况下搜查嫌疑人的手机。","C":"在未经授权的情况下检查嫌疑人的手机内容。","D":"禁止嫌疑人使用其手机。"},"analysis":{"type":"The Supreme Court will work out whether 后面接的是一件具体、可在某段落直接对应的事实，没有 infer/imply/mainly 等信号。","parse":"The Supreme Court will work out whether, during an arrest, it is legitimate to____ 主干是 The Supreme Court will work out whether...；whether 引导宾语从句，从句主语用形式主语 it，真正主语是后面的不定式（即四个选项接入处）；during an arrest 是插入语，仅做时间限定，不影响主干。","locateEn":"The Supreme Court will now consider whether police can search the contents of a mobile phone without a warrant if the phone is on or around a person during an arrest.","locateZh":"最高法院现在将审议：如果手机在被捕人身上或身边，警察是否可以在没有搜查令的情况下搜查手机内容。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"prevent suspects from deleting their phone contents 说的是「防止嫌疑人删手机内容」，这在 they could take reasonable measures to ensure that phone data are not erased or altered while waiting for a warrant 中是后文提到的「等令状期间警方的配套措施」，不是最高法院本案要裁决的问题。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"search for suspects' mobile phones without a warrant 的 search for（寻找）+ mobile phones（手机本身）说的是「找出嫌疑人的手机」，而原文 search the contents of a mobile phone 是「搜手机里的内容」，宾语层级被偷换。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"预判要求出现「查手机内容 + 未获授权」两个语义模块；C 项 check suspects' phone contents without being authorized 两块都精确对译，是定位句的同义替换，锁定 C。","pairs":[{"orig":"search the contents of a mobile phone","opt":"check suspects' phone contents"},{"orig":"without a warrant","opt":"without being authorized"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"prohibit suspects from using their mobile phones 谈的是「禁止嫌疑人使用手机」，全文从未出现「禁止使用」这层意思，本案讨论的是「搜查」而非「使用禁令」。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":27,"stem":"The author's attitude toward California's argument is one of____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"disapproval."},{"key":"B","text":"indifference."},{"key":"C","text":"tolerance."},{"key":"D","text":"cautiousness."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"态度题","stemZh":"作者对加利福尼亚州论点的态度是","choicesZh":{"A":"不赞成。","B":"漠不关心。","C":"容忍。","D":"谨慎。"},"analysis":{"type":"The author's attitude toward California's argument is one of____ 中 attitude 一词直接亮明题型。","parse":"The author's attitude toward California's argument is one of____ 主系表结构：主语是 The author's attitude；toward California's argument 是后置介宾短语，限定态度的针对对象；is one of ____ 是「态度是……之一」的固定句式，选项填入名词性的情感词。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"预判极性为「强烈负面」，A 项 disapproval 是「不赞同」的标准态度名词，正好匹配 lame、recklessly modest、should not swallow whole 共同构成的负面情感共识。","pairs":[{"orig":"California's lame argument","opt":"disapproval"},{"orig":"should not swallow California's argument whole","opt":"disapproval"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"indifference 表示「漠不关心」，但作者整篇都在主动反驳 California 并提出替代方案（Enough of the implications are discernable, even obvious），是高度投入的批评者而非旁观者。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"tolerance 是「容忍/可以接受但不喜欢」的弱负面态度；本文作者用 lame argument 这种带嘲讽意味的形容词，并主张 should not swallow California's argument whole，是强烈反对而非「勉强容忍」。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"cautiousness 描述的是「小心谨慎、不轻易下结论」的态度；但作者在第 3 段就直接断言 Enough of the implications are discernable, even obvious，毫不犹豫地下了判断，第 4 段更用 lame 这种直接的贬义词——并不谨慎，而是相当干脆地否定。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":28,"stem":"The author believes that exploring one's phone contents is comparable to____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"going through one's wallet."},{"key":"B","text":"handling one's historical records."},{"key":"C","text":"scanning one's correspondences."},{"key":"D","text":"getting into one's residence."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"作者认为探查一个人的手机内容可以比作","choicesZh":{"A":"翻查一个人的钱包。","B":"处理一个人的历史记录。","C":"扫描一个人的信件。","D":"进入一个人的住所。"},"analysis":{"type":"exploring one's phone contents is comparable to____ 问的是文中已明说的比拟对象，可在某段中直接锁定。","parse":"The author believes that exploring one's phone contents is comparable to____ 主干 The author believes that ...；that 引导宾语从句，从句主语是动名词短语 exploring one's phone contents，谓语 is comparable to，宾语位置由选项的动名词短语填充——要求选项与主语在语法形态上对齐（都用 -ing 形式）。","locateEn":"But exploring one's smartphone is more like entering his or her home.","locateZh":"但探查一个人的智能手机更像是闯入他或她的家。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"going through one's wallet 直接对应 going through a suspect's purse——但原文这是 California 提出、被作者用 But 否定的类比，不是作者本人认可的比拟对象。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"handling one's historical records 中的 historical records 对应原文 reading history, financial history, medical history——这些是手机里**装的东西**（用以解释为什么像家），不是作者用来类比的**比拟对象**。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"scanning one's correspondences 对应原文 comprehensive records of recent correspondence——也是手机里**装的东西**之一，作者并未把「查手机」类比为「翻通讯记录」。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判要求选「住宅/家」类的比拟对象；D 项 getting into one's residence 与 entering his or her home 是几乎一一对应的近义替换，锁定 D。","pairs":[{"orig":"entering his or her home","opt":"getting into one's residence"}]}}}}},{"n":29,"stem":"In Paragraphs 5 and 6, the author shows his concern that____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"principles are hard to be clearly expressed."},{"key":"B","text":"the court is giving police less room for action."},{"key":"C","text":"phones are used to store sensitive information."},{"key":"D","text":"citizens' privacy is not effectively protected."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"在第5段和第6段中,作者表达了他的担忧","choicesZh":{"A":"原则很难被清楚地表达。","B":"法院正在给予警方更少的行动空间。","C":"手机被用来存储敏感信息。","D":"公民的隐私没有得到有效保护。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干已指定段落，问作者具体担忧什么，答案在两段中直接可寻——本质是细节题，只是答题域跨两段。","parse":"In Paragraphs 5 and 6, the author shows his concern that____ 主句 the author shows his concern；that 引导同位语从句，补充说明 concern 的具体内容（即选项内容是「担忧」的具体所指）；句首 In Paragraphs 5 and 6 是地点状语，限定证据范围。","locateEn":"Citizens still have a right to expect private documents to remain private and protected by the Constitution's prohibition on unreasonable searches.","locateZh":"公民依然有权期待私人文件保持私密、并受到宪法对不合理搜查之禁令的保护。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"principles are hard to be clearly expressed 把原文 stating that principle doesn't ease the challenge of line-drawing 篡改为「原则难以清晰表达」；原文说的是「原则容易讲、边界难划」，难点不在「表达原则」而在「划定执行边界」。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"the court is giving police less room for action 说「法院在缩小警方行动空间」，但 The court, though, may want to allow room for police to cite situations where they are entitled to more freedom. 明确表态「法院可能要为警察留出空间」——方向完全相反。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"phones are used to store sensitive information 对应 keeping sensitive information on these devices is increasingly a requirement of normal life——但作者此处只是中性陈述「这是现代生活的必需」，并未把「存敏感信息于手机」本身当成担忧的对象。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判落点是「隐私名义有保障但实际未落地」，D 项 citizens' privacy is not effectively protected 恰好用「未被有效保护」一词概括了 still…right（应然）与 doesn't ease the challenge（实然难落地）之间的鸿沟，且情感极性为负面，与 concern 完全对齐。","pairs":[{"orig":"Citizens still have a right to expect private documents to remain private and protected","opt":"citizens' privacy is not effectively protected"},{"orig":"stating that principle doesn't ease the challenge of line-drawing","opt":"citizens' privacy is not effectively protected"}]}}}}},{"n":30,"stem":"Orin Kerr's comparison is quoted to indicate that____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"the Constitution should be implemented flexibly."},{"key":"B","text":"principles of the Constitution should never be altered."},{"key":"C","text":"California's argument violates principles of the Constitution."},{"key":"D","text":"new technology requires reinterpretation of the Constitution."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"例证目的题","stemZh":"引用Orin Kerr的比较是为了表明","choicesZh":{"A":"宪法应该灵活实施。","B":"宪法原则永远不应被改变。","C":"加利福尼亚州的论点违反了宪法原则。","D":"新技术需要对宪法进行重新解释。"},"analysis":{"type":"Orin Kerr's comparison is quoted to indicate that____ 中 quoted to indicate 是例证目的题的标准提问。","parse":"Orin Kerr's comparison is quoted to indicate that____ 被动句：主语是 Orin Kerr's comparison；is quoted 是被动语态，省略的施事是作者；to indicate that... 是不定式作目的状语；that 引导宾语从句填入选项内容。整句意思是「作者引用 Kerr 的比较，目的是表明……」。","locateEn":"New, disruptive technology sometimes demands novel applications of the Constitution's protections. Orin Kerr, a law professor, compares the explosion and accessibility of digital information in the 21st century with the establishment of automobile use as a virtual necessity of life in the 20th: The justices had to specify novel rules for the new personal domain of the passenger car then; they must sort out how the Fourth Amendment applies to digital information now.","locateZh":"崭新的颠覆性技术有时要求对宪法保护作出新颖的适用。法学教授 Orin Kerr 把 21 世纪数字信息的爆炸与可及性，与 20 世纪汽车成为生活必需的确立相对比：那时大法官们必须为乘客车厢这一新私人领域专门制定新规；如今他们必须厘清第四修正案如何适用于数字信息。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"the Constitution should be implemented flexibly 用 flexibly 把作者的「针对新技术作出 novel applications」泛化成了「灵活实施」；原文强调的不是执行方式的弹性，而是面对新事物时**释义/适用**需要更新——一个层级偏低、一个层级偏高。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"principles of the Constitution should never be altered 说「宪法原则永远不应被改动」，但作者明确主张需要 novel applications of the Constitution's protections——「新的适用」本质上正是对原则的延伸/再解释，与「永远不变」相反。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"California's argument violates principles of the Constitution 把第 7 段开头对 California 的反对（the justices should not swallow California's argument whole）拿来冒充 Kerr 引用的目的；但 Kerr 的引用本身只服务于「新技术需新适用」这一上位论点，并不直接评价 California 是否「违反宪法原则」。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判要求选「新技术 → 宪法需新适用」一类表述，D 项 new technology requires reinterpretation of the Constitution 直接对应作者论点句，不困在 Kerr 引用的「汽车」「乘客车厢」等论据细节里，是例证目的题的标准正确项。","pairs":[{"orig":"New, disruptive technology sometimes demands novel applications of the Constitution's protections","opt":"new technology requires reinterpretation of the Constitution"}]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-3","kind":"reading","title":"Text 3","textNo":3,"directions":"","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 3","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-1","text":"The journal Science is adding an extra round of statistical checks to its peer-review process, editor-in-chief Marcia McNutt announced today. The policy follows similar efforts from other journals, after widespread concern that basic mistakes in data analysis are contributing to the irreproducibility of many published research findings."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-2","text":"\"Readers must have confidence in the conclusions published in our journal,\" writes McNutt in an editorial. Working with the American Statistical Association, the journal has appointed seven experts to a statistics board of reviewing editors (SBoRE). Manuscripts will be flagged up for additional scrutiny by the journal's internal editors, or by its existing Board of Reviewing Editors or by outside peer reviewers. The SBoRE panel will then find external statisticians to review these manuscripts."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-3","text":"Asked whether any particular papers had impelled the change, McNutt said: \"The creation of the 'statistics board' was motivated by concerns broadly with the application of statistics and data analysis in scientific research and is part of Science's overall drive to increase reproducibility in the research we publish.\""},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-4","text":"Giovanni Parmigiani, a biostatistician at the Harvard School of Public Health, a member of the SBoRE group, says he expects the board to \"play primarily an advisory role.\" He agreed to join because he \"found the foresight behind the establishment of the SBoRE to be novel, unique and likely to have a lasting impact. This impact will not only be through the publications in Science itself, but hopefully through a larger group of publishing places that may want to model their approach after Science.\""},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-5","text":"John Ioannidis, a physician who studies research methodology, says that the policy is \"a most welcome step forward\" and \"long overdue\". \"Most journals are weak in statistical review, and this damages the quality of what they publish. I think that, for the majority of scientific papers nowadays, statistical review is more essential than expert review,\" he says, but he noted that biomedical journals such as Annals of Internal Medicine, the Journal of the American Medical Association and The Lancet pay strong attention to statistical review."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-6","text":"Professional scientists are expected to know how to analyse data, but statistical errors are alarmingly common in published research, according to David Vaux, a cell biologist. Researchers should improve their standards, he wrote in 2012, but journals should also take a tougher line, \"engaging reviewers who are statistically literate and editors who can verify the process\". Vaux says that Science's idea to pass some papers to statisticians \"has some merit, but a weakness is that it relies on the board of reviewing editors to identify 'the papers that need scrutiny' in the first place\"."}]},"questions":[{"n":31,"stem":"It can be learned from Paragraph 1 that____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Science intends to simplify its peer-review process."},{"key":"B","text":"journals are strengthening their statistical checks."},{"key":"C","text":"few journals are blamed for mistakes in data analysis."},{"key":"D","text":"lack of data analysis is common in research projects."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"从第一段可以得知____","choicesZh":{"A":"Science 期刊打算简化其同行评审流程。","B":"期刊正在加强其统计审查。","C":"很少有期刊因数据分析错误而受到指责。","D":"缺乏数据分析在研究项目中很常见。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干It can be learned from Paragraph 1 that要求从指定段落中读出一个客观信息，而非作出推论或归纳全篇。","parse":"题干It can be learned from Paragraph 1 that____是被动语态的形式主语句（learned 的逻辑宾语就是后面 that 从句），结构平铺，无陷阱。","locateEn":"The policy follows similar efforts from other journals, after widespread concern that basic mistakes in data analysis are contributing to the irreproducibility of many published research findings.","locateZh":"在人们普遍担忧数据分析中的基本错误正导致大量已发表研究无法复现的背景下，这一政策（指 Science 新增统计审查）是在效仿其他期刊已有的类似做法。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文是adding an extra round of statistical checks（增加额外一轮审查），方向是『加强』；选项 A 却说 simplify（简化），与原文意思完全相反。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"预判要求『多家期刊都在加强统计审查』，B 项主语 journals（复数）+谓语 are strengthening + 宾语 statistical checks 三要素逐一对应，是唯一不偏不漏的选项。","pairs":[{"orig":"The policy follows similar efforts from other journals","opt":"journals are strengthening their statistical checks"},{"orig":"adding an extra round of statistical checks","opt":"statistical checks"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文是widespread concern（广泛担忧），暗示『许多』期刊都被诟病；选项 C 用 few（几乎没有）反向缩小，与 widespread 矛盾。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文说的是mistakes in data analysis（数据分析中犯错），即『做了但做错』；选项 D 却说 lack of data analysis（缺少数据分析），即『压根没做』，两者不是一回事。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":32,"stem":"The phrase \"flagged up\" (Para. 2) is the closest in meaning to____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"found."},{"key":"B","text":"revised."},{"key":"C","text":"marked."},{"key":"D","text":"stored."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义句意题","stemZh":"短语\"flagged up\"(第二段)在意思上最接近____","choicesZh":{"A":"发现。","B":"修订。","C":"标记。","D":"存储。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干The phrase \"flagged up\" (Para. 2) is the closest in meaning to明确询问某短语的近义，是典型的词义题模板。","parse":"题干为简单系表结构，主语是带引号的短语，谓语 is，表语 the closest in meaning to + 选项。无难点。","locateEn":"Manuscripts will be flagged up for additional scrutiny by the journal's internal editors, or by its existing Board of Reviewing Editors or by outside peer reviewers.","locateZh":"稿件将被期刊内部编辑、已有的评审编辑委员会或外部同行评议者『flagged up』，以便接受额外审查。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"found（被发现）暗示『此前未知，现被找到』；但原文 flagged up 的对象是已经进入评审流程的稿件，它们并不需要『被发现』，而是『被标出来再查』。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"revised 意为『修改、修订』，原文 flagged up 阶段还没有任何修改动作，只是『标出待查』；修改要等到后续 SBoRE 审完之后才可能发生。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"预判得到『被挑出、被标记』，四个选项中只有 marked 能精确承载『标出』这层含义，其余三项在『使其引起注意』这一核心义上均不对位。","pairs":[{"orig":"flagged up for additional scrutiny","opt":"marked"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"stored（被存储）意味着『放进档案/保存起来』，与『为额外审查而标出』方向完全不同——前者是收档静置，后者是引起注意。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":33,"stem":"Giovanni Parmigiani believes that the establishment of the SBoRE may____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"pose a threat to all its peers."},{"key":"B","text":"meet with strong opposition."},{"key":"C","text":"increase Science's circulation."},{"key":"D","text":"set an example for other journals."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"Giovanni Parmigiani 认为 SBoRE 的建立可能会____","choicesZh":{"A":"对其所有同行构成威胁。","B":"遭遇强烈反对。","C":"增加 Science 期刊的发行量。","D":"为其他期刊树立榜样。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干Giovanni Parmigiani believes that要求复述某人原话的客观含义，是细节题里的观点归属型。","parse":"主语是人名，谓语 believes 后接 that 从句作宾语；从句主语 the establishment of the SBoRE 是动名词短语，重点是题干为「主语 + 情态动词 may」的开放式延续，选项要接得通顺。","locateEn":"This impact will not only be through the publications in Science itself, but hopefully through a larger group of publishing places that may want to model their approach after Science.","locateZh":"这种影响不仅体现在《Science》自身的发表上，更有望让更多刊物以《Science》为榜样、效仿它的做法。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文novel, unique and likely to have a lasting impact是清一色褒义；选项 A 用 threat（威胁）把同行关系反转为对立性负面，与全段语气完全相反。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"Parmigiani 整段都在正面赞扬这一举措，原文未出现任何 opposition / resistance / criticism 字样；『遭遇强烈反对』在原文中没有任何依据。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文谈的是『方法/做法被效仿』，落点在学术影响力；选项 C 谈的是『发行量增加』，落点在商业销量，两件事在原文中没有任何联系。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判要求『为他人立榜样』，D 项 set an example for other journals 把 model... after Science 完整翻译成了立榜样的固定搭配，是四个选项中唯一同向同强度的表达。","pairs":[{"orig":"a larger group of publishing places that may want to model their approach after Science","opt":"set an example for other journals"},{"orig":"a larger group of publishing places","opt":"other journals"}]}}}}},{"n":34,"stem":"David Vaux holds that what Science is doing now____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"adds to researchers' workload."},{"key":"B","text":"diminishes the role of reviewers."},{"key":"C","text":"has room for further improvement."},{"key":"D","text":"is to fail in the foreseeable future."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"细节题","stemZh":"David Vaux 认为 Science 期刊目前所做的____","choicesZh":{"A":"增加了研究人员的工作量。","B":"削弱了审稿人的作用。","C":"仍有进一步改进的空间。","D":"在可预见的未来注定会失败。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干David Vaux holds that what Science is doing now问的是 Vaux 对 Science 现行做法的看法，本质仍是观点归属型细节题。","parse":"题干主句是 David Vaux holds that... ；从句主语 what Science is doing now 是 what 引导的名词性从句作 holds 的宾语从句的主语，重点要看清谁是观点持有者（Vaux）、谁是被评价对象（Science 的做法）。","locateEn":"Vaux says that Science's idea to pass some papers to statisticians \"has some merit, but a weakness is that it relies on the board of reviewing editors to identify 'the papers that need scrutiny' in the first place\".","locateZh":"Vaux 说，Science 把部分论文交由统计学家审阅的做法『有可取之处，但弱点在于它首先依赖评审编辑委员会去识别出 \"需要进一步审查的论文\"』。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文中 Vaux 抱怨的是『识别可疑论文的环节有漏洞』，他从未谈及研究者的工作量；workload（工作量）在原文里没有任何对应表述。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"Vaux 的批评是『评审编辑可能选不准要被审的论文』，他想要的是更强的评审能力，而非削弱评审者作用；diminishes the role of reviewers（削弱评审者作用）与他立场相反。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"预判要求『既承认价值又指出弱点』，C 项 has room for further improvement 同时容纳了肯定（已有部分价值）与保留（还可改进）两层语义，是唯一不偏不漏的归纳。","pairs":[{"orig":"has some merit, but a weakness is that it relies on the board of reviewing editors to identify 'the papers that need scrutiny' in the first place","opt":"has room for further improvement"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"Vaux 只是指出一个弱点，未对未来下定论；选项 D 却把『有弱点』升格为 fail in the foreseeable future（在可预见的未来必将失败），强度严重过头。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":35,"stem":"Which of the following is the best title of the text?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Science Joins Push to Screen Statistics in Papers"},{"key":"B","text":"Professional Statisticians Deserve More Respect"},{"key":"C","text":"Data Analysis Finds Its Way onto Editors' Desks"},{"key":"D","text":"Statisticians Are Coming Back with Science"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"主旨题","stemZh":"以下哪项是本文的最佳标题?","choicesZh":{"A":"Science 期刊加入推动论文统计审查的行列","B":"专业统计学家应获得更多尊重","C":"数据分析进入编辑的办公桌","D":"统计学家与 Science 期刊再度携手"},"analysis":{"type":"题干Which of the following is the best title of the text?是标准主旨题模板，要求选项概括全篇核心事件。","parse":"标准疑问句，which of the following + 系动词 is + 表语 the best title of the text，无难点。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"A 项同时具备『事件主体 Science』『核心动作 Screen Statistics』『行业背景 Joins Push』三要素，这是其他三项任何一个都不具备的完整覆盖。","pairs":[{"orig":"Science is adding an extra round of statistical checks","opt":"Science Joins Push to Screen Statistics in Papers"},{"orig":"follows similar efforts from other journals","opt":"Joins Push"},{"orig":"Manuscripts will be flagged up for additional scrutiny","opt":"Screen Statistics in Papers"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"全文谈的是『Science 给论文加统计审查』这件事，没有任何一段在论证『统计学家配不配获得更多尊重』；B 项的主语 Professional Statisticians 与全文真正主语 Science 错位。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"Data Analysis Finds Its Way onto Editors' Desks 字面意为『数据分析进入编辑案头』，既未点明事件主体 Science，也偷换了对象——文中是把论文送给『外部统计学家』审阅，并不是把数据分析活儿塞给编辑做。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"Statisticians Are Coming Back with Science 暗示『统计学家在归来/卷土重来』，原文从未说过统计学家曾被踢出局或正在回归；这是凭空捏造的『回归』叙事。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-4","kind":"reading","title":"Text 4","textNo":4,"directions":"","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 4","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-1","text":"Two years ago, Rupert Murdoch's daughter, Elisabeth, spoke of the \"unsettling dearth of integrity across so many of our institutions\". Integrity had collapsed, she argued, because of a collective acceptance that the only \"sorting mechanism\" in society should be profit and the market. But \"it's us, human beings, we the people who create the society we want, not profit.\""},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-2","text":"Driving her point home, she continued: \"It's increasingly apparent that the absence of purpose, of a moral language within government, media or business could become one of the most dangerous goals for capitalism and freedom.\" This same absence of moral purpose was wounding companies such as News International, she thought, making it more likely that it would lose its way as it had with widespread illegal telephone hacking."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-3","text":"As the hacking trial concludes – finding guilty one ex-editor of the News of the World, Andy Coulson, for conspiring to hack phones, and finding his predecessor, Rebekah Brooks, innocent of the same charge – the wider issue of dearth of integrity still stands. Journalists are known to have hacked the phones of up to 5,500 people. This is hacking on an industrial scale, as was acknowledged by Glenn Mulcaire, the man hired by the News of the World in 2001 to be the point person for phone hacking. Others await trial. This long story still unfolds."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-4","text":"In many respects, the dearth of moral purpose frames not only the fact of such widespread phone hacking but the terms on which the trial took place. One of the astonishing revelations was how little Rebekah Brooks knew of what went on in her newsroom, how little she thought to ask and the fact that she never inquired how the stories arrived. The core of her successful defence was that she knew nothing."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-5","text":"In today's world, it has become normal that well-paid executives should not be accountable for what happens in the organisations that they run. Perhaps we should not be so surprised. For a generation, the collective doctrine has been that the sorting mechanism of society should be profit. The words that have mattered are efficiency, flexibility, shareholder value, business-friendly, wealth generation, sales, impact and, in newspapers, circulation. Words degraded to the margin have been justice, fairness, tolerance, proportionality and accountability."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-6","text":"The purpose of editing the News of the World was not to promote reader understanding, to be fair in what was written or to betray any common humanity. It was to ruin lives in the quest for circulation and impact. Ms Brooks may or may not have had suspicions about how her journalists got their stories, but she asked no questions, gave no instructions – nor received traceable, recorded answers."}]},"questions":[{"n":36,"stem":"According to the first two paragraphs, Elisabeth was upset by____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"the consequences of the current sorting mechanism."},{"key":"B","text":"companies' financial loss due to immoral practices."},{"key":"C","text":"governmental ineffectiveness on moral issues."},{"key":"D","text":"the wide misuse of integrity among institutions."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据前两段,Elisabeth感到不安的原因是____","choicesZh":{"A":"当前筛选机制造成的后果。","B":"公司因不道德行为导致的财务损失。","C":"政府在道德问题上的无效作为。","D":"诚信在各机构中的广泛滥用。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 According to the first two paragraphs, Elisabeth was upset by____ 用 According to 限定来源，再问让她不安的「东西」，是对原文事实的客观转述，不是 infer/imply。","parse":"题干 According to the first two paragraphs, Elisabeth was upset by____ 是一个被动语态的省略陈述句，主干 Elisabeth was upset by X，X 即正解；选项作 by 的宾语补全。无陷阱。","locateEn":"Integrity had collapsed, she argued, because of a collective acceptance that the only \"sorting mechanism\" in society should be profit and the market.","locateZh":"她主张，诚信之所以崩塌，是因为社会形成了一种集体共识——认为唯一的「筛选机制」就只能是利润与市场。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"预判的「机制导致的恶果」与 A 的「现行筛选机制的后果」在因果方向、主客双方都一一对应，是定位句最干净的同义改写。","pairs":[{"orig":"Integrity had collapsed, she argued, because of a collective acceptance that the only \"sorting mechanism\" in society should be profit and the market","opt":"the consequences of the current sorting mechanism"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"companies' financial loss due to immoral practices 把焦点偷换成「不道德行为导致公司财务损失」——wounding companies such as News International 讲的是「失去方向 (lose its way)」而非财务亏损，且这是公司层面的后果，不是 Elisabeth 不安的根因。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"governmental ineffectiveness on moral issues 把原文 within government, media or business 三方并列的「道德语言缺失」窄化到只剩政府层面的「无作为」，是典型的以偏概全。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"the wide misuse of integrity among institutions 说 integrity 被「广泛滥用」，但原文是 unsettling dearth of integrity，即 integrity 严重缺失而非被滥用，方向恰好相反。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":37,"stem":"It can be inferred from Paragraph 3 that____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Glenn Mulcaire may deny phone hacking as a crime."},{"key":"B","text":"more journalists may be found guilty of phone hacking."},{"key":"C","text":"Andy Coulson should be held innocent of the charge."},{"key":"D","text":"phone hacking will be accepted on certain occasions."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"推断题","stemZh":"从第三段可以推断出____","choicesZh":{"A":"Glenn Mulcaire可能否认电话窃听是犯罪行为。","B":"可能会有更多记者被判电话窃听罪。","C":"Andy Coulson应被判定无罪。","D":"电话窃听在某些情况下会被接受。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 It can be inferred from Paragraph 3 that____ 以 inferred from 起句，是推断题的标准形态。","parse":"It can be inferred from Paragraph 3 that____ 是 it 形式主语句，真正主语是 that 引导的名词性从句（即四个选项），结构平实，无陷阱。","locateEn":"Journalists are known to have hacked the phones of up to 5,500 people. This is hacking on an industrial scale, as was acknowledged by Glenn Mulcaire, the man hired by the News of the World in 2001 to be the point person for phone hacking. Others await trial.","locateZh":"已知有记者侵入了多达 5500 人的电话；这是工业级规模的窃听，被 2001 年受雇为窃听负责人的 Glenn Mulcaire 本人所承认；还有其他人正在候审。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"Glenn Mulcaire may deny phone hacking as a crime 说他可能否认窃听是犯罪，但原文 as was acknowledged by Glenn Mulcaire 恰恰说他「承认了」这是工业级窃听，且文中没有任何关于他是否把它看作犯罪的话——属于无中生有 + 与原文相反的复合错。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"把 Others await trial 的事实嫁接到 may be found guilty 的可能性，再用 hacking on an industrial scale 暗示「不止一人」，三处共同支撑 B；用 may 而非 will 也守住了推断的一步。","pairs":[{"orig":"Others await trial. This long story still unfolds","opt":"more journalists may be found guilty of phone hacking"},{"orig":"hacking on an industrial scale","opt":"more journalists"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"Andy Coulson should be held innocent of the charge 主张应判 Coulson 无罪，但原文 finding guilty one ex-editor of the News of the World, Andy Coulson, for conspiring to hack phones 明确他已被判有罪——这是与原文事实的正面冲突。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"phone hacking will be accepted on certain occasions 说窃听在某些场合会被接受，但原文从头到尾把窃听描述为 industrial scale 的犯罪行为；这个结论是凭空推到两步之外的过度延伸。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":38,"stem":"The author believes that Rebekah Brooks's defence____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"was hardly convincing."},{"key":"B","text":"centered on trivial issues."},{"key":"C","text":"revealed a cunning personality."},{"key":"D","text":"was part of a conspiracy."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"态度题","stemZh":"作者认为Rebekah Brooks的辩护____","choicesZh":{"A":"几乎没有说服力。","B":"集中在琐碎问题上。","C":"暴露了狡猾的性格。","D":"是阴谋的一部分。"},"analysis":{"type":"The author believes that Rebekah Brooks's defence____ 问的是作者对一个具体对象（defence）的评价，选项 was hardly convincing revealed a cunning personality 也都是评价性形容，是局部态度题。","parse":"主干 The author believes that ...，that 引导宾语从句，从句主语 Rebekah Brooks's defence 后接横线，等待补全表语；选项是对该 defence 的整体判断。无陷阱。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"作者对 Brooks 的 defence 给出 astonishing 的负向评价，与 A 的「难以令人信服」在对象（defence）与极性（负向）上完全吻合。","pairs":[{"orig":"One of the astonishing revelations was how little Rebekah Brooks knew of what went on in her newsroom","opt":"was hardly convincing"},{"orig":"she never inquired how the stories arrived","opt":"hardly convincing"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"centered on trivial issues 说辩护「围绕琐碎议题展开」，但原文从未评价辩护的「议题大小」，只评价它「以无知为核心」；trivial 完全是无中生有。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"revealed a cunning personality 说她辩护暴露出狡猾的性格，但原文只评价了她的「不闻不问」这一行为及其辩护策略，并未给她下「狡猾」这种人格判断——这是过度推断到人格层面。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"was part of a conspiracy 把她的辩护定性为「阴谋的一部分」，原文只说 conspiring to hack phones 是 Coulson 的罪名，没有把 Brooks 的辩护行为本身归为共谋的一环——属于无中生有兼张冠李戴。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":39,"stem":"The author holds that the current collective doctrine shows","options":[{"key":"A","text":"a marginalized lifestyle."},{"key":"B","text":"unfair wealth distribution."},{"key":"C","text":"generally distorted values."},{"key":"D","text":"a rigid moral code."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"推断题","stemZh":"作者认为当前的集体信条显示出","choicesZh":{"A":"边缘化的生活方式。","B":"不公平的财富分配。","C":"普遍扭曲的价值观。","D":"僵化的道德准则。"},"analysis":{"type":"The author holds that the current collective doctrine shows____ 用 shows 要求从原文对该 doctrine 的事实描述（推崇什么词、贬抑什么词）推断它「显示出了什么」，需要一步抽象归纳。","parse":"主干 The author holds that [the current collective doctrine] shows ____。that 引导宾语从句，从句主语是名词短语 the current collective doctrine，shows 后接选项作宾语。","locateEn":"For a generation, the collective doctrine has been that the sorting mechanism of society should be profit. The words that have mattered are efficiency, flexibility, shareholder value, business-friendly, wealth generation, sales, impact and, in newspapers, circulation. Words degraded to the margin have been justice, fairness, tolerance, proportionality and accountability.","locateZh":"整整一代人的集体信条都是：社会的筛选机制应当是利润。被奉为圭臬的词是效率、灵活、股东价值、亲商、财富创造、销量、影响力以及报纸里的发行量；被贬到边缘的词，则是正义、公平、宽容、比例、问责。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"a marginalized lifestyle 说显示出一种「被边缘化的生活方式」，但原文 degraded to the margin 是用来形容那些「正义、公平」等词的，而不是生活方式；把修饰对象偷换成 lifestyle 是张冠李戴。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"unfair wealth distribution 把焦点定到「财富分配不公」，但原文 wealth generation 只是「被推崇的词之一」，关于这份财富在社会成员间如何分配，原文一字未提——典型的无中生有。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"把两组对立的词清单整体归纳成「价值观被普遍扭曲」，正是 generally distorted values 所做的事；扭曲一词的语义与原文「margin/should be profit」的倾斜分布完全契合。","pairs":[{"orig":"The words that have mattered are efficiency, flexibility, shareholder value, business-friendly, wealth generation, sales, impact and, in newspapers, circulation","opt":"generally distorted values"},{"orig":"Words degraded to the margin have been justice, fairness, tolerance, proportionality and accountability","opt":"generally distorted"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"a rigid moral code 说显示出一种「刻板的道德准则」，但原文核心是「道德语言缺失、正义公平被边缘化」（absence of purpose, of a moral language + Words degraded to the margin…justice, fairness…accountability）——根本没有道德准则在场，更谈不上「刻板」。方向反了。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":40,"stem":"Which of the following is suggested in the last paragraph?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The quality of writings is of primary importance."},{"key":"B","text":"Moral awareness matters in editing a newspaper."},{"key":"C","text":"Common humanity is central to news reporting."},{"key":"D","text":"Journalists need stricter industrial regulations."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"推断题","stemZh":"最后一段暗示了以下哪一项?","choicesZh":{"A":"写作质量至关重要。","B":"道德意识在编辑报纸时很重要。","C":"共同的人性是新闻报道的核心。","D":"记者需要更严格的行业监管。"},"analysis":{"type":"Which of the following is suggested in the last paragraph? 用 suggested 求尾段隐含的作者主张，是推断题。","parse":"Which of the following is suggested in the last paragraph? 是被动疑问句，主语 which 指代四个选项，is suggested 表「被暗示」，状语 in the last paragraph 限定范围。无陷阱。","locateEn":"The purpose of editing the News of the World was not to promote reader understanding, to be fair in what was written or to betray any common humanity. It was to ruin lives in the quest for circulation and impact.","locateZh":"编辑《世界新闻报》的目的，并非促进读者理解、并非追求笔下公正、也并非守住基本人性；它的目的，是为了追逐发行量与影响力而毁人前程。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"The quality of writings is of primary importance 把焦点放在「写作质量」上，但原文谈的是道德取向缺失（公正 / 人性 / 读者理解），而非文章写得好不好——把道德议题偷换成质量议题。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"把「应有却缺席」的三个道德要素和「实际所做」的恶行并置，作者实质上在主张：编辑这一行为应有道德意识——正是 B 的命题。","pairs":[{"orig":"The purpose of editing the News of the World was not to promote reader understanding, to be fair in what was written or to betray any common humanity","opt":"Moral awareness matters in editing a newspaper"},{"orig":"It was to ruin lives in the quest for circulation and impact","opt":"Moral awareness matters in editing a newspaper"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"Common humanity is central to news reporting 只抓住 common humanity 一项，将其单挑成 central to news reporting；但原文 not to promote reader understanding, to be fair in what was written or to betray any common humanity 是三项并列、共同构成道德要求，选 C 等于以偏概全。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"Journalists need stricter industrial regulations 主张「需要更严格的行业监管」，但原文未对监管/法规做任何呼吁，作者诉求是道德层面的「编辑要有道德意识」，而非制度层面的「加强外部监管」——属于过度推断到制度层面。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-b","kind":"matching","title":"Section II Part B 选句填空","directions":"Directions: In the following text, some sentences have been removed. For Questions 41-45, choose the most suitable one from the list A-G to fit into each of the numbered blanks. There are two extra choices, which do not fit in any of the blanks. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET. (10 points)","points":10,"passage":{"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-b-1","label":"","text":"How does your reading proceed? Clearly you try to comprehend, in the sense of identifying meanings for individual words and working out relationships between them, drawing on your implicit knowledge of English grammar. {{41}} You begin to infer a context for the text, for instance by making decisions about what kind of speech event is involved: Who is making the utterance, to whom, when and where."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-2","label":"","text":"The ways of reading indicated here are without doubt kinds of comprehension. But they show comprehension to consist not just of passive assimilation but of active engagement in inference and problem-solving. You infer information you feel the writer has invited you to grasp by presenting you with specific evidence and clues. {{42}}"},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-3","label":"","text":"Conceived in this way, comprehension will not follow exactly the same track for each reader. What is in question is not the retrieval of an absolute, fixed or \"true\" meaning that can be read off and checked for accuracy, or some timeless relation of the text to the world. {{43}}"},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-4","label":"","text":"Such background material inevitably reflects who we are. {{44}} This doesn't, however, make interpretation merely relative or even pointless. Precisely because readers from different historical periods, places and social experiences produce different but overlapping readings of the same words on the page–including for texts that engage with fundamental human concerns – debates about texts can play an important role in social discussion of beliefs and values."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-5","label":"","text":"How we read a given text also depends to some extent on our particular interest in reading it. {{45}} Such dimensions of reading suggest – as others introduced later in the book will also do – that we bring an implicit (often unacknowledged) agenda to any act of reading. It doesn't then necessarily follow that one kind of reading is fuller, more advanced or more worthwhile than another. Ideally, different kinds of reading inform each other, and act as useful reference points for and counterbalances to one another. Together, they make up the reading component of your overall literacy, or relationship to your surrounding textual environment."}]},"choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Are we studying that text and trying to respond in a way that fulfills the requirement of a given course? Reading it simply for pleasure? Skimming it for information? Ways of reading on a train or in bed are likely to differ considerably from reading in a seminar room."},{"key":"B","text":"Factors such as the place and period in which we are reading, our gender, ethnicity, age and social class will encourage us towards certain interpretations but at the same time obscure or even close off others."},{"key":"C","text":"If you are unfamiliar with words or idioms, you guess at their meaning, using clues presented in the context. On the assumption that they will become relevant later, you make a mental note of discourse entities as well as possible links between them."},{"key":"D","text":"In effect, you try to reconstruct the likely meanings or effects that any given sentence, image or reference might have had: These might be the ones the author intended."},{"key":"E","text":"You make further inferences, for instance, about how the text may be significant to you, or about its validity – inferences that form the basis of a personal response for which the author will inevitably be far less responsible."},{"key":"F","text":"In plays, novels and narrative poems, characters speak as constructs created by the author, not necessarily as mouthpieces for the author's own thoughts."},{"key":"G","text":"Rather, we ascribe meanings to texts on the basis of interaction between what we might call textual and contextual material: between kinds of organization or patterning we perceive in a text's formal structures (so especially its language structures) and various kinds of background, social knowledge, belief and attitude that we bring to the text."}],"questions":[{"n":41,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Are we studying that text and trying to respond in a way that fulfills the requirement of a given course? Reading it simply for pleasure? Skimming it for information? Ways of reading on a train or in bed are likely to differ considerably from reading in a seminar room."},{"key":"B","text":"Factors such as the place and period in which we are reading, our gender, ethnicity, age and social class will encourage us towards certain interpretations but at the same time obscure or even close off others."},{"key":"C","text":"If you are unfamiliar with words or idioms, you guess at their meaning, using clues presented in the context. On the assumption that they will become relevant later, you make a mental note of discourse entities as well as possible links between them."},{"key":"D","text":"In effect, you try to reconstruct the likely meanings or effects that any given sentence, image or reference might have had: These might be the ones the author intended."},{"key":"E","text":"You make further inferences, for instance, about how the text may be significant to you, or about its validity – inferences that form the basis of a personal response for which the author will inevitably be far less responsible."},{"key":"F","text":"In plays, novels and narrative poems, characters speak as constructs created by the author, not necessarily as mouthpieces for the author's own thoughts."},{"key":"G","text":"Rather, we ascribe meanings to texts on the basis of interaction between what we might call textual and contextual material: between kinds of organization or patterning we perceive in a text's formal structures (so especially its language structures) and various kinds of background, social knowledge, belief and attitude that we bring to the text."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"段中","stemZh":"41. (41)____","reason":"If you are unfamiliar with words or idioms, you guess at their meaning, using clues presented in the context. On the assumption that they will become relevant later, you make a mental note of discourse entities as well as possible links between them. 如果你不熟悉某些词汇或习语,你会利用上下文中呈现的线索来猜测它们的含义。基于它们稍后会变得相关的假设,你会在心里记下话语实体以及它们之间可能存在的联系。\n夹在\"识词推关系\"与\"开始推语境\"之间，需补一层基础阅读动作 空前句讲 identifying meanings for individual words and working out relationships between them, drawing on your implicit knowledge of English grammar——即识别单词意义、理清词间关系；空后句 You begin to infer a context for the text——开始推断语境。这个段中空夹在两个\"阅读动作\"之间，要补一句仍属\"识词/记关系\"这一基础层、并能自然过渡到\"推语境\"的动作描述。\n猜词义+记实体关系，正落识词层、又铺垫推语境 C 句 If you are unfamiliar with words or idioms, you guess at their meaning, using clues presented in the context 与空前 identifying meanings for individual words 同属\"处理词义\"，clues / context 还与空后 infer a context 的词呼应；后半 you make a mental note of discourse entities as well as possible links between them 又与前句 working out relationships between them 对上。主语都是 you，承上启下严丝合缝。","options":{},"choiceEn":"If you are unfamiliar with words or idioms, you guess at their meaning, using clues presented in the context. On the assumption that they will become relevant later, you make a mental note of discourse entities as well as possible links between them.","clues":["词汇复现 前句的 meanings / clues / context 应在正确句里复现 空前句的核心词是 identifying meanings for individual words 和 working out relationships between them，空后句又出现 infer a context。这条线索要求正确句要继续围绕\"词义、语境线索、词间联系\"这套词来说，属于第1段动作清单的同一词汇场。","结构功能 段中位置要求承接\"识词\"、铺垫\"推语境\" 由 working out relationships between them 到 You begin to infer a context 这一跳，结构上需要一句仍以 you 为主语、描述\"遇生词怎么办\"的动作句来过渡，正确句须承担这种顺接功能。"]}},{"n":42,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Are we studying that text and trying to respond in a way that fulfills the requirement of a given course? Reading it simply for pleasure? Skimming it for information? Ways of reading on a train or in bed are likely to differ considerably from reading in a seminar room."},{"key":"B","text":"Factors such as the place and period in which we are reading, our gender, ethnicity, age and social class will encourage us towards certain interpretations but at the same time obscure or even close off others."},{"key":"C","text":"If you are unfamiliar with words or idioms, you guess at their meaning, using clues presented in the context. On the assumption that they will become relevant later, you make a mental note of discourse entities as well as possible links between them."},{"key":"D","text":"In effect, you try to reconstruct the likely meanings or effects that any given sentence, image or reference might have had: These might be the ones the author intended."},{"key":"E","text":"You make further inferences, for instance, about how the text may be significant to you, or about its validity – inferences that form the basis of a personal response for which the author will inevitably be far less responsible."},{"key":"F","text":"In plays, novels and narrative poems, characters speak as constructs created by the author, not necessarily as mouthpieces for the author's own thoughts."},{"key":"G","text":"Rather, we ascribe meanings to texts on the basis of interaction between what we might call textual and contextual material: between kinds of organization or patterning we perceive in a text's formal structures (so especially its language structures) and various kinds of background, social knowledge, belief and attitude that we bring to the text."}],"answer":"E","explanation":{"answer":"E","type":"段末","stemZh":"42. (42)____","reason":"You make further inferences, for instance, about how the text may be significant to you, or about its validity – inferences that form the basis of a personal response for which the author will inevitably be far less responsible. 你作出进一步的推断,例如关于文本对你可能具有的意义,或关于其有效性的推断——这些推断构成了个人回应的基础,而作者对此必然要承担少得多的责任。\n段末空，要在\"从证据线索推断\"之上再补一句收束 空前句讲 You infer information you feel the writer has invited you to grasp by presenting you with specific evidence and clues——你从作者给的证据线索里推断信息。这是本段末尾，空要补一句在\"推断\"基础上再推进/收束的话，自然延续\"主动推断\"的论点。\nfurther inferences 递进收束，且引出\"作者少负责\"接下段 E 句 You make further inferences 用 further 紧承前句的\"一层推断\"再加一层，inferences 与 You infer information 同根复现；句末 a personal response for which the author will inevitably be far less responsible 把话题推向\"个人化回应、作者少负责\"，恰好为第3段\"意义不因人而固定\"铺路。双向缝合。","options":{},"choiceEn":"You make further inferences, for instance, about how the text may be significant to you, or about its validity – inferences that form the basis of a personal response for which the author will inevitably be far less responsible.","clues":["词汇复现 前句 infer / information 应在正确句里再现 空前句 You infer information you feel the writer has invited you to grasp 反复用 infer/information，要求正确句继续谈\"推断\"，最好出现 inference(s) 这一同根词以形成复现。","逻辑连接 需要\"再进一层\"的递进逻辑 前句已经描述了一层推断，段末若要收束，常见走向是\"在此基础上还会作更进一步的推断\"。这条线索要求正确句带递进意味（如 further 一类），承接 You infer information 而非另起。"]}},{"n":43,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Are we studying that text and trying to respond in a way that fulfills the requirement of a given course? Reading it simply for pleasure? Skimming it for information? Ways of reading on a train or in bed are likely to differ considerably from reading in a seminar room."},{"key":"B","text":"Factors such as the place and period in which we are reading, our gender, ethnicity, age and social class will encourage us towards certain interpretations but at the same time obscure or even close off others."},{"key":"C","text":"If you are unfamiliar with words or idioms, you guess at their meaning, using clues presented in the context. On the assumption that they will become relevant later, you make a mental note of discourse entities as well as possible links between them."},{"key":"D","text":"In effect, you try to reconstruct the likely meanings or effects that any given sentence, image or reference might have had: These might be the ones the author intended."},{"key":"E","text":"You make further inferences, for instance, about how the text may be significant to you, or about its validity – inferences that form the basis of a personal response for which the author will inevitably be far less responsible."},{"key":"F","text":"In plays, novels and narrative poems, characters speak as constructs created by the author, not necessarily as mouthpieces for the author's own thoughts."},{"key":"G","text":"Rather, we ascribe meanings to texts on the basis of interaction between what we might call textual and contextual material: between kinds of organization or patterning we perceive in a text's formal structures (so especially its language structures) and various kinds of background, social knowledge, belief and attitude that we bring to the text."}],"answer":"G","explanation":{"answer":"G","type":"段末","stemZh":"43. (43)____","reason":"Rather, we ascribe meanings to texts on the basis of interaction between what we might call textual and contextual material: between kinds of organization or patterning we perceive in a text's formal structures (so especially its language structures) and various kinds of background, social knowledge, belief and attitude that we bring to the text. 相反,我们是在文本材料与语境材料之间的互动基础上赋予文本以意义:即我们在文本的形式结构(尤其是其语言结构)中感知到的各种组织或模式,与我们带入文本的各种背景、社会知识、信念和态度之间的互动。\n段末空，前面刚否定一种意义观，需补\"而是……\"的正面替代 空前句 What is in question is not the retrieval of an absolute, fixed or \"true\" meaning that can be read off and checked for accuracy——明确否定\"意义是固定可核对的真义\"。段末这一空要补的，正是与这个否定相对的正面主张：那意义到底是怎么来的。\nRather 反转 + 抛出 background material 供下段 Such 回指 G 句 Rather, we ascribe meanings to texts on the basis of interaction between what we might call textual and contextual material：Rather 正面反转前句的 not 否定，再提 meanings 形成复现；句末 various kinds of background, social knowledge, belief and attitude that we bring to the text 抛出 background material，正好被第4段段首 Such background material 的 Such 回指。前后双向锁死。","options":{},"choiceEn":"Rather, we ascribe meanings to texts on the basis of interaction between what we might call textual and contextual material: between kinds of organization or patterning we perceive in a text's formal structures (so especially its language structures) and various kinds of background, social knowledge, belief and attitude that we bring to the text.","clues":["逻辑连接 前句 not 句强烈呼唤一个 Rather/Instead 的反转 空前 What is in question is not the retrieval of an absolute, fixed or \"true\" meaning 是典型\"not X\"结构，按英语论证习惯，紧接着应有 \"Rather/Instead, Y\" 给出正面替代。这条线索要求正确句句首带 Rather 一类反转词。","词汇复现 正确句须再提 meaning，并引出供下段回指的 background material 前句核心是 meaning fixed or \"true\" meaning，正确句应继续讲 meaning 如何产生；同时第4段段首 Such background material inevitably reflects who we are 用 Such 回指，说明本空句里必须先出现 \"background material\" 这个名词供其指代。"]}},{"n":44,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Are we studying that text and trying to respond in a way that fulfills the requirement of a given course? Reading it simply for pleasure? Skimming it for information? Ways of reading on a train or in bed are likely to differ considerably from reading in a seminar room."},{"key":"B","text":"Factors such as the place and period in which we are reading, our gender, ethnicity, age and social class will encourage us towards certain interpretations but at the same time obscure or even close off others."},{"key":"C","text":"If you are unfamiliar with words or idioms, you guess at their meaning, using clues presented in the context. On the assumption that they will become relevant later, you make a mental note of discourse entities as well as possible links between them."},{"key":"D","text":"In effect, you try to reconstruct the likely meanings or effects that any given sentence, image or reference might have had: These might be the ones the author intended."},{"key":"E","text":"You make further inferences, for instance, about how the text may be significant to you, or about its validity – inferences that form the basis of a personal response for which the author will inevitably be far less responsible."},{"key":"F","text":"In plays, novels and narrative poems, characters speak as constructs created by the author, not necessarily as mouthpieces for the author's own thoughts."},{"key":"G","text":"Rather, we ascribe meanings to texts on the basis of interaction between what we might call textual and contextual material: between kinds of organization or patterning we perceive in a text's formal structures (so especially its language structures) and various kinds of background, social knowledge, belief and attitude that we bring to the text."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"段中","stemZh":"44. (44)____","reason":"Factors such as the place and period in which we are reading, our gender, ethnicity, age and social class will encourage us towards certain interpretations but at the same time obscure or even close off others. 诸如我们所处的阅读地点和时期、我们的性别、种族、年龄和社会阶层等因素会促使我们倾向于某些解读,但同时也会模糊甚至完全阻断其他解读。\n承\"背景反映我们是谁\"，启\"但这不使解读变得相对无意义\" 空前句 Such background material inevitably reflects who we are——背景必然反映我们是谁；空后句 This doesn't, however, make interpretation merely relative or even pointless——但这并不使解读变得相对或无意义。这个段中空要补一句进一步说明\"背景如何作用于解读\"的话，好让空后的 This 和 however 有可转折、可指代的对象。\n具体化背景因素并道出\"既引导又封死解读\"，正供 This 与 however 转折 B 句 Factors such as the place and period in which we are reading, our gender, ethnicity, age and social class 正是对前句 Such background material 的具体展开；句末 obscure or even close off others 给出\"背景会封死某些解读\"这一略显消极的论断，恰好是空后 This doesn't, however, make interpretation merely relative or even pointless 用 This 回指、用 however 让步反转的对象。三句一气贯通。","options":{},"choiceEn":"Factors such as the place and period in which we are reading, our gender, ethnicity, age and social class will encourage us towards certain interpretations but at the same time obscure or even close off others.","clues":["代词指代 空后 This 需要本空句提供先行内容 空后 This doesn't, however, make interpretation merely relative or even pointless 的 This 是单数指示代词，必须回指空里那句话的内容。这条线索要求正确句给出一个\"会让人担心解读变相对/无意义\"的论断，供 This 指代。","词汇复现 正确句须延续 background / who we are 的话题 空前 Such background material inevitably reflects who we are 把话题定在\"背景因素塑造我们\"，正确句应继续具体化这些背景因素（地点、时代、身份等），与 who we are 同场。"]}},{"n":45,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Are we studying that text and trying to respond in a way that fulfills the requirement of a given course? Reading it simply for pleasure? Skimming it for information? Ways of reading on a train or in bed are likely to differ considerably from reading in a seminar room."},{"key":"B","text":"Factors such as the place and period in which we are reading, our gender, ethnicity, age and social class will encourage us towards certain interpretations but at the same time obscure or even close off others."},{"key":"C","text":"If you are unfamiliar with words or idioms, you guess at their meaning, using clues presented in the context. On the assumption that they will become relevant later, you make a mental note of discourse entities as well as possible links between them."},{"key":"D","text":"In effect, you try to reconstruct the likely meanings or effects that any given sentence, image or reference might have had: These might be the ones the author intended."},{"key":"E","text":"You make further inferences, for instance, about how the text may be significant to you, or about its validity – inferences that form the basis of a personal response for which the author will inevitably be far less responsible."},{"key":"F","text":"In plays, novels and narrative poems, characters speak as constructs created by the author, not necessarily as mouthpieces for the author's own thoughts."},{"key":"G","text":"Rather, we ascribe meanings to texts on the basis of interaction between what we might call textual and contextual material: between kinds of organization or patterning we perceive in a text's formal structures (so especially its language structures) and various kinds of background, social knowledge, belief and attitude that we bring to the text."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"段中","stemZh":"45. (45)____","reason":"Are we studying that text and trying to respond in a way that fulfills the requirement of a given course? Reading it simply for pleasure? Skimming it for information? Ways of reading on a train or in bed are likely to differ considerably from reading in a seminar room. 我们是在研究那篇文本并试图以满足特定课程要求的方式作出回应吗?仅仅是为了愉悦而阅读它?还是为了获取信息而略读它?在火车上或床上的阅读方式很可能与在研讨室里的阅读方式大不相同。\n承\"读法取决于阅读兴趣\"，启\"这些读法维度说明我们带着隐含目的\" 空前句 How we read a given text also depends to some extent on our particular interest in reading it——怎么读取决于我们读它的具体兴趣；空后句 Such dimensions of reading suggest ... that we bring an implicit (often unacknowledged) agenda to any act of reading——这些阅读维度表明我们带着隐含的目的。空要补一句把\"兴趣\"具体化为几种不同读法/目的，好让空后 Such dimensions of reading 有所指。\n连串设问列举多种读法目的，正好被 Such dimensions of reading 回指 A 句 Are we studying that text ...? Reading it simply for pleasure? Skimming it for information? 一连列出研读、消遣、略读等多种按目的区分的读法，正是对空前 our particular interest in reading it 的具体化；这\"若干种读法\"恰好就是空后 Such dimensions of reading 中 Such 复数所回指的对象；末句 Ways of reading on a train or in bed are likely to differ considerably from reading in a seminar room 再次强调读法因场合而异，话题完全咬合。","options":{},"choiceEn":"Are we studying that text and trying to respond in a way that fulfills the requirement of a given course? Reading it simply for pleasure? Skimming it for information? Ways of reading on a train or in bed are likely to differ considerably from reading in a seminar room.","clues":["代词指代 空后 Such dimensions of reading 需本空句先列出多种读法 空后 Such dimensions of reading suggest 的 Such + 复数 dimensions 必须回指空里给出的\"若干种读法/维度\"。这条线索要求正确句列举出多种不同的阅读方式或目的。","词汇复现 正确句须围绕 interest / purpose 的不同读法 空前 our particular interest in reading it 把话题定在\"读的兴趣/目的\"，正确句应顺着具体化为\"为应付课程读、为消遣读、为找信息读\"这类按目的区分的读法。"]}}]},{"id":"section2-part-c","kind":"translation","title":"Section II Part C 英译汉","directions":"Directions: Read the following text carefully and then translate the underlined segments into Chinese. Write your answers on the ANSWER SHEET. ( 10 points)","points":10,"passage":{"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-c-1","text":"Within the span of a hundred years, in the seventeenth and early eighteenth centuries, a tide of emigration – one of the great folk wanderings of history – swept from Europe to America. (46) This movement, driven by powerful and diverse motivations, built a nation out of a wilderness and, by its nature, shaped the character and destiny of an uncharted continent."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-2","text":"(47) The United States is the product of two principal forces – the immigration of European peoples with their varied ideas, customs, and national characteristics and the impact of a new country which modified these traits. Of necessity, colonial America was a projection of Europe. Across the Atlantic came successive groups of Englishmen, Frenchmen, Germans, Scots, Irishmen, Dutchmen, Swedes, and many others who attempted to transplant their habits and traditions to the new world. (48) But the force of geographic conditions peculiar to America, the interplay of the varied national groups upon one another, and the sheer difficulty of maintaining old-world ways in a raw, new continent caused significant changes. These changes were gradual and at first scarcely visible. But the result was a new social pattern which, although it resembled European society in many ways, had a character that was distinctly American."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-3","text":"(49) The first shiploads of immigrants bound for the territory which is now the United States crossed the Atlantic more than a hundred years after the 15th- and 16th-century explorations of North America. In the meantime, thriving Spanish colonies had been established in Mexico, the West Indies, and South America. These travelers to North America came in small, unmercifully overcrowded craft. During their six- to twelve-week voyage, they survived on barely enough food allotted to them. Many of the ships were lost in storms, many passengers died of disease, and infants rarely survived the journey. Sometimes storms blew the vessels far off their course, and often calm brought unbearably long delay."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-4","text":"To the anxious travelers the sight of the American shore brought almost inexpressible relief. Said one recorder of events, \"The air at twelve leagues' distance smelt as sweet as a new-blown garden.\" The colonists' first glimpse of the new land was a sight of dense woods. (50) The virgin forest with its richness and variety of trees was a real treasure-house which extended from Maine all the way down to Georgia. Here was abundant fuel and lumber. Here was the raw material of houses and furniture, ships and potash, dyes and naval stores."}]},"sentences":[{"n":46,"en":"This movement, driven by powerful and diverse motivations, built a nation out of a wilderness and, by its nature, shaped the character and destiny of an uncharted continent.","refZh":"这场移民浪潮，在各种强大而多样的动机的驱动下，在荒野上建立起一个国家，并就其本质而言，塑造了这片未知大陆的特征与命运。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"This movement,","lit":"这场移民（浪潮）","role":"（主语）","note":"难点： This movement 要回指还原 movement 在这里不是「运动」，而是承接上一句 a tide of emigration，指「这场移民浪潮」。先回上句把它还原成「移民」，整句的主语才落得稳。"},{"en":"driven by powerful and diverse motivations,","lit":"由强大而多样的动机所驱动的","role":"（过去分词短语作后置定语（修饰 movement））","note":"难点： 被动分词作定语，提前到主语前译 driven by… 是过去分词短语，回头修饰 This movement，相当于一个「（被）…驱动的」定语。译时把它提到主语前：「在…驱动下的这场移民」或「这场移民…由…驱动」。diverse 取「多种多样的」，别漏译。"},{"en":"built a nation out of a wilderness","lit":"在一片荒野上建立起一个国家","role":"（谓语1 + 宾语 + 介词短语）","note":"难点： build … out of … 是「用…造出…」 build A out of B 意为「从 B 中造出 A / 在 B 的基础上建起 A」。wilderness 是「荒野、未开垦之地」，照基调取中性义。整块译「在荒野上 / 从荒野中建起一个国家」。"},{"en":"and, by its nature,","lit":"而且，就其本质而言，","role":"（并列连词 + 插入语）","note":"难点： by its nature 是插入语 by its nature 夹在两个谓语之间，是插入语，意为「就其本性 / 从本质上说」。翻译时把它归位到第二个谓语前，不要硬塞在 and 后读不通。"},{"en":"shaped the character and destiny of an uncharted continent.","lit":"塑造了一片未知大陆的性格与命运","role":"（谓语2 + 宾语）","note":"难点： uncharted = 未被勘测的、未知的 uncharted 字面是「未绘入地图的」，引申为「未经探索的、陌生的」。character 这里指（大陆的）「特性、风貌」而非「人物性格」，可译「特征 / 风貌」。整块：塑造了这片未知大陆的特征与命运。"}]},{"n":47,"en":"The United States is the product of two principal forces – the immigration of European peoples with their varied ideas, customs, and national characteristics and the impact of a new country which modified these traits.","refZh":"美国是两种主要力量的产物：一是欧洲各民族带着各自不同的思想、习俗和民族特性的移民，二是一个新国家的影响，这个新国家改变了上述这些特征。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"The United States is the product of two principal forces –","lit":"美国是两种主要力量的产物——","role":"（主句主谓表）","note":"难点： 破折号引出「两种力量」的具体内容 two principal forces（两种主要力量）后面的破折号，引出的是对「这两种力量到底是什么」的具体解释。看懂破折号是「下面要展开说明」的信号，后面那一长串就好归类了。"},{"en":"the immigration of European peoples with their varied ideas, customs, and national characteristics","lit":"带着各自不同思想、习俗和民族特性的欧洲各民族的移民","role":"（破折号后第一项（force 一））","note":"难点： peoples 复数 = 各民族；with 短语作定语 people 加 s 变 peoples，意思从「人们」变成「民族、种族」，这里指「欧洲各（个）民族」。with their varied ideas… 是介词短语作定语，修饰 European peoples，译「带着 / 拥有各自不同思想、习俗和民族特性的」。这是第一种力量。"},{"en":"and the impact of a new country","lit":"以及一个新国家的影响","role":"（破折号后第二项（force 二））","note":"难点： 这个 and 才是「两种力量」的并列 句中有两个 and：前一个（ideas, customs, and national characteristics）只是名词列举；这一个 and 连接的才是 the immigration… 和 the impact… 两大并列项，正对应前面说的 two principal forces。切对这个 and，「两种力量」才数得清。"},{"en":"which modified these traits.","lit":"（这个新国家）改变了这些特征","role":"（which 定语从句（修饰 a new country））","note":"难点： these traits 回指 + modified 取「改变」 these traits 指前面的 their varied ideas, customs, and national characteristics，即移民带来的「这些（思想习俗等）特征」。modify 在这里不是「修饰」，而是「改变 / 改造」。which 引导的定语从句修饰 a new country，可译「这个新国家改变了这些特征」。"}]},{"n":48,"en":"But the force of geographic conditions peculiar to America, the interplay of the varied national groups upon one another, and the sheer difficulty of maintaining old-world ways in a raw, new continent caused significant changes.","refZh":"但是，美国特有的地理条件所产生的作用、各民族群体之间的相互影响，以及在原始的新大陆上维持旧世界生活方式所面临的极大困难，共同造成了重大的变化。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"But the force of geographic conditions peculiar to America,","lit":"但美国特有的地理条件的影响（力量）","role":"（并列主语之一）","note":"难点： peculiar to = 为…所特有的 peculiar 在这里不是「古怪的」，而是搭配 peculiar to America，意为「美国特有的 / 美国所独有的」。peculiar to… 是固定搭配「为…所特有」。force 这里指地理条件造成的「作用、影响力」。"},{"en":"the interplay of the varied national groups upon one another,","lit":"各民族群体彼此之间的相互影响","role":"（并列主语之二）","note":"难点： interplay … upon one another = 相互作用 interplay 是「相互作用、相互影响」；upon one another（= upon each other）意为「彼此对彼此」，与 interplay 呼应，强调「各民族群体之间的相互影响」。one another 指代「各 national groups 彼此」。"},{"en":"and the sheer difficulty of maintaining old-world ways in a raw, new continent","lit":"以及在原始的新大陆上维持旧世界生活方式的极大困难","role":"（并列主语之三）","note":"难点： sheer 表程度、old-world ways 指旧大陆习俗 sheer 这里是「纯粹的、十足的」，用来加强 difficulty 的程度，可译「极大的 / 简直难以…」。old-world ways 指「旧世界（欧洲）的生活方式 / 习俗」，raw 修饰 continent 取「未开发的、原始的」中性义，别译成「生的」。"},{"en":"caused significant changes.","lit":"造成了重大变化","role":"（谓语 + 宾语）","note":"难点： 这是三个并列主语共用的谓语 caused significant changes 是整句唯一的谓语，前面三个用逗号和 and 并列的名词短语都是它的主语。看清「三主语共一谓语」，才不会把句子读散。"}]},{"n":49,"en":"The first shiploads of immigrants bound for the territory which is now the United States crossed the Atlantic more than a hundred years after the 15th- and 16th-century explorations of North America.","refZh":"最早的几批移民乘船驶往如今已是美国的那片领土，他们横渡大西洋的时间，比十五、十六世纪对北美的勘探晚了一百多年。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"The first shiploads of immigrants","lit":"第一批一船船的移民","role":"（主语）","note":"难点： shiploads = 一船船（的量） shipload 是「一船（的货 / 人）」，复数 shiploads of immigrants 指「一船船的移民、整船整船的移民」。The first shiploads 即「最早的几批（成船的）移民」。别把 shiploads 漏看成 ships。"},{"en":"bound for the territory","lit":"驶往那片领土的","role":"（过去分词短语作后置定语（修饰 immigrants））","note":"难点： be bound for = 前往、开往 bound 在这里不是「捆绑」或「跳跃」，bound for 是固定搭配「驶往、开往、前往某地」。bound for the territory 作后置定语修饰 immigrants，意为「驶往那片领土的（移民）」。"},{"en":"which is now the United States","lit":"（这片领土）如今就是美国","role":"（which 定语从句（修饰 territory））","note":"难点： 短定语从句前移成「如今的…」 which is now the United States 修饰 the territory，从句很短，汉语提到 territory 前译成「如今已是美国的那片领土」或「即今日美国所在的那片土地」。"},{"en":"crossed the Atlantic","lit":"横渡了大西洋","role":"（谓语 + 宾语）","note":"难点： 全句真正的谓语在这里 主语 immigrants 被 bound for… which is now… 一长串定语隔开，谓语 crossed 一直拖到后面才出现。认准 crossed 是主句谓语，主干就是「移民横渡了大西洋」。"},{"en":"more than a hundred years after the 15th- and 16th-century explorations of North America.","lit":"在十五、十六世纪对北美的探索之后一百多年","role":"（时间状语）","note":"难点： more than a hundred years after = 在…之后一百多年 这是时间状语：以 the 15th- and 16th-century explorations of North America（十五、十六世纪对北美的勘探）为基准，crossing 发生在它「之后一百多年」。explorations 这里指「探索、勘探（活动）」。"}]},{"n":50,"en":"The virgin forest with its richness and variety of trees was a real treasure-house which extended from Maine all the way down to Georgia.","refZh":"这片树木繁茂、种类繁多的原始森林是一座名副其实的宝库，它从缅因州一直绵延到南边的佐治亚州。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"The virgin forest","lit":"那片原始森林","role":"（主语）","note":"难点： virgin = 原始的、未开发的 virgin 在这里不是常见的字面义，virgin forest 是固定说法「原始森林、处女林」，指「未经砍伐 / 未被开发的森林」，呼应全文对新大陆「原始、未开发」的中性描写（a raw, new continent）。"},{"en":"with its richness and variety of trees","lit":"树木繁茂、种类繁多的","role":"（with 介词短语作后置定语（修饰 forest））","note":"难点： 抽象名词具体化：richness/variety→形容词 richness（丰富）和 variety（多样）是抽象名词，直译「树木的丰富和多样」生硬。具体化译成形容词短语：「树木繁茂、种类繁多」，更像中文。这是抽象名词具体化的典型处理。"},{"en":"was a real treasure-house","lit":"是一座名副其实的宝库","role":"（系动词 + 表语）","note":"难点： real treasure-house 取「名副其实的宝库」 treasure-house 是「宝库、宝藏之地」；real 修饰它，取「名副其实的、真正的」，强调这片森林确实物产丰饶（下文 Here was abundant fuel and lumber 正是佐证）。是比喻说法，译「宝库」即可。"},{"en":"which extended from Maine all the way down to Georgia.","lit":"（它）一直绵延从缅因州到佐治亚州","role":"（which 定语从句（修饰 treasure-house/forest））","note":"难点： extend … all the way down to = 一直延伸到 extend from A all the way down to B 意为「从 A 一路延伸 / 绵延到 B」。Maine（缅因州）在北、Georgia（佐治亚州）在南，down 体现自北向南，可译「从缅因州一直绵延到南边的佐治亚州」。which 从句较长，宜单独译成分句。"}]}]},{"id":"section3-part-a","kind":"writing","title":"Section III Part A 写作","directions":"51. Directions: You are going to host a club reading session. Write an email of about 100 words recommending a book to the club members. You should state reasons for your recommendation. You should write neatly on the ANSWER SHEET. Do not sign your own name at the end of the letter, use “Li Ming ” instead. Do not write the address. (10 points)","prompt":"51. Directions: You are going to host a club reading session. Write an email of about 100 words recommending a book to the club members. You should state reasons for your recommendation. You should write neatly on the ANSWER SHEET. Do not sign your own name at the end of the letter, use “Li Ming ” instead. Do not write the address. (10 points)","points":10,"image":null,"model":{"essay":"Dear club members, As the host of this reading session, I am delighted to recommend a wonderful book for us to read together: The Old Man and the Sea by Ernest Hemingway. I choose it for two reasons. First, the story is simple yet deeply moving, telling how an old fisherman fights a giant fish alone and never gives up. Second, its plain but powerful language shows us that a man can be destroyed but not defeated, which truly inspires our growth. I hope you will enjoy it and share your thoughts in our discussion. Yours, Li Ming","translation":"亲爱的俱乐部成员们： 作为本次读书会的主持人，我很高兴向大家推荐一本值得共读的好书：海明威的《老人与海》。\n我推荐它有两个理由。第一，故事简单却极为动人，讲述了一位老渔夫如何独自与一条大鱼搏斗、永不放弃。第二，它朴素而有力的语言让我们看到\"人可以被毁灭，但不能被打败\"，这真正启发着我们的成长。\n希望大家喜欢这本书，并在我们的讨论中分享你们的想法。 此致 李明","breakdown":[{"tag":"【第 1 段】","core":"开篇亮身份并抛出书名","text":"【第 1 段】 开篇亮身份并抛出书名 。用 As the host of this reading session, I am delighted to recommend... 一句同时报身份、表来意、点出书名 The Old Man and the Sea，落实了第 1 段\"开门见山亮身份与来意\"的方法。"},{"tag":"【第 2 段】","core":"First/Second 分点且各带具体细节","text":"【第 2 段】 First/Second 分点且各带具体细节 。用 I choose it for two reasons. First,... Second,... 搭出两条骨架，再各配 telling how an old fisherman fights a giant fish 与 a man can be destroyed but not defeated 这样的具体说明，兑现了主体段\"先分点再各配一句具体说明\"。"},{"tag":"【第 3 段】","core":"礼貌邀请加规范落款","text":"【第 3 段】 礼貌邀请加规范落款 。以 I hope you will enjoy it and share your thoughts in our discussion 礼貌收束并发出号召，末尾另起一行落款 Li Ming、不写地址，落实第 3 段方法与署名硬约束。"}],"wordCount":98}},{"id":"section3-part-b","kind":"writing","title":"Section III Part B 写作","directions":"52. Directions: Write an essay of 160-200 words based on the following picture. In your essay, you should 1）describe the picture briefly, 2）interpret its intended meaning, and 3）give your comments. You should write neatly on ANSWER SHEET. (20 points)","prompt":"52. Directions: Write an essay of 160-200 words based on the following picture. In your essay, you should 1）describe the picture briefly, 2）interpret its intended meaning, and 3）give your comments. You should write neatly on ANSWER SHEET. (20 points)","points":20,"image":{"src":"assets/writing/2015-english-one-section3-part-b.jpg","alt":"这是一幅标题为“手机时代的聚会”的黑白漫画","width":"634","height":"532","local":"assets/writing/2015-english-one-section3-part-b.jpg"},"model":{"essay":"As is vividly depicted in the cartoon, four people are seated around a round table set with several dishes, chopsticks and cups. Yet instead of chatting, each of them lowers their head, staring at a mobile phone, and no one says a single word. The title, 'A Gathering in the Age of Mobile Phones,' makes the scene both familiar and thought-provoking. Simple as it is, the picture conveys a profound message about modern life. What the artist intends to criticize is the way smartphones have quietly invaded our face-to-face communication. People who sit together physically are actually worlds apart emotionally, as their attention is captured by the tiny screens rather than the friends beside them. The warmth that a gathering is supposed to bring is thus replaced by cold silence. As far as I am concerned, such a phenomenon deserves our alarm. Convenient though smartphones are, they should never become a barrier between us and those we love. Therefore, measures should be taken: we ought to put our phones aside during gatherings and cherish real conversation. Only in this way can technology serve us instead of enslaving us.","translation":"正如漫画生动描绘的那样，四个人围坐在一张摆着几盘菜、几双筷子和几个杯子的圆桌旁。然而他们并不交谈，每个人都低着头，盯着手机，没有人说一句话。‘手机时代的聚会’这个标题让这一幕既熟悉又发人深省。\n画面虽简单，却传递出关于现代生活的深刻信息。作者真正想批评的，是智能手机悄然侵蚀了我们面对面的交流。那些身体坐在一起的人，情感上其实相隔千里，因为他们的注意力被小小的屏幕夺走，而非身边的朋友。聚会本该带来的温情，就这样被冰冷的沉默所取代。\n在我看来，这一现象值得我们警觉。智能手机固然便利，但绝不该成为我们与所爱之人之间的一道屏障。因此，我们应当采取措施：聚会时把手机放到一边，珍惜真实的交谈。唯有如此，技术才能服务于人，而非奴役人。","breakdown":[{"tag":"【第 1 段】","core":"客观描述且点题制造反差","text":"【第 1 段】 客观描述且点题制造反差 。本段用‘As is vividly depicted in the cartoon’总起，再用‘Yet instead of chatting, each of them lowers their head’的 instead of 句架把‘本该交流却各看手机’的反差写足，全程现在时、只写看得见的要素，最后用 title 点题却不解释寓意——正是描述段方法的落地。"},{"tag":"【第 2 段】","core":"从画面引申到社会现象","text":"【第 2 段】 从画面引申到社会现象 。本段先以‘Simple as it is, the picture conveys a profound message’过渡，再用‘What the artist intends to criticize is’直接点明批评对象，接着‘People who sit together physically are actually worlds apart emotionally’把具象画面翻译成现实道理，完成了采分核心的引申。"},{"tag":"【第 3 段】","core":"表态让步再给对策","text":"【第 3 段】 表态让步再给对策 。本段以‘As far as I am concerned’亮态度，用‘Convenient though smartphones are’让步显客观，再以‘measures should be taken’引出放下手机、珍惜交谈的具体做法，末句展望收束，兑现了评论段的表态加对策方法。"}],"wordCount":188}}],"questionCount":45,"objectiveCount":45,"answerTable":{"ans-section1":"完形填空（四选一） Section I （1–20 题） 1 D what · 2 B concluded · 3 C on · 4 A compared · 5 C samples · 6 A insignificant · 7 D know · 8 A resemble · 9 B also · 10 D Perhaps · 11 B to · 12 A drive · 13 B rather than · 14 D benefits · 15 C faster · 16 C understand · 17 B contributory · 18 D tendency · 19 C ethnic · 20 A see \n（逐题解析见 完形填空（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-1":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (1) （21–25 题） 21 D · 22 C · 23 B · 24 A · 25 C \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-2":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (2) （26–30 题） 26 C · 27 A · 28 D · 29 D · 30 D \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-3":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (3) （31–35 题） 31 B · 32 C · 33 D · 34 C · 35 A \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-4":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (4) （36–40 题） 36 A · 37 B · 38 A · 39 C · 40 B \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-b":"选句填空（7选5） Section II Part B （41–45 题） 41 C · 42 E · 43 G · 44 B · 45 A \n（逐题解析见 选句填空（7选5）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-c":"英译汉（划线句子翻译） Section II Part C \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 英译汉（划线句子翻译）解析页","ans-section3-part-a":"写作 Section III Part A \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 写作解析页","ans-section3-part-b":"写作 Section III Part B \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 写作解析页"},"coverage":{"objective":45,"explained":45,"missingSections":[]}};
